window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2017-12-2", "source": "2017年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay commenting on the saying \"Seek to understand others,and you will be understood \". You can cite examples to illustrate your views. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The saying \"Seek to understand others, and you will be understood\" reveals a simple but profound truth about\nhuman relationships. I firmly agree with this view, because understanding is reciprocal: only when we make\nthe effort to grasp another person's feelings and position will others be willing to open their hearts and\nunderstand us in return.\nTake the workplace as an example. A manager who bothers to listen to an employee's real difficulties, rather\nthan rushing to give orders, earns that employee's trust and cooperation. The same holds true between parents\nand children. When a father sets aside his prejudices and truly listens to his teenage daughter, she no longer\nfeels judged and gradually shares her honest thoughts. In both cases, the act of understanding others, by\nlistening, putting oneself in their shoes and dropping assumptions, builds a bridge that lets understanding\nflow back.\nTherefore, being understood is not something we can demand; it is something we earn by giving understanding\nfirst. If each of us takes the initiative to step into others' shoes, mutual understanding will follow naturally, and\nour relationships will grow warmer and stronger."}, "translation": {"prompt": "青海湖位于海拔3,205米、青海省省会西宁以西约100公里处，是中国最大的咸水湖，面积4,317平方公里，最深处25.5米。有23条河注入湖中，其中大部分是季节性的。百分之八十的湖水源于五条主要河流。青海湖位于跨越亚洲的几条候鸟迁徙路线的交叉处。许多鸟类把青海湖作为迁徙过程中的暂息地。湖的西侧是著名的\"鸟岛\"，吸引着来自世界各地的观鸟者。每年夏天，游客们也来这里观看国际自行车比赛。", "sample": "Located at an altitude of 3,205 meters and about 100 kilometers west of Xining, the capital of Qinghai Province, Qinghai Lake is the largest saltwater lake in China, with an area of 4,317 square kilometers and a maximum depth of 25.5 meters. There are 23 rivers flowing into the lake, most of which are seasonal. Eighty percent of the lake's water comes from five major rivers. Qinghai Lake lies at the crossroads of several bird migration routes spanning Asia. As a result, many birds use it as a stopover during their migration. On the western side of the lake lies the famous Bird Island, attracting birdwatchers from all over the world. Every summer, tourists also come here to watch the international cycling race."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2017-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The Pacific island nation of Palau has become home to the sixth largest marine sanctuary in the world. The new marine reserve, now the largest in the Pacific, will  __26__  no fishing or mining. Palau also established the world's first shark sanctuary in 2009.\nThe tiny island nation has set aside 500,000 square kilometres—80 percent—of its maritime  __27__  , for full protection. That's the highest percentage of an  __28__  economic zone devoted to marine conservation by any country in the world. The remaining 20 percent of the Palau seas will be reserved for local fishing by individuals and small-scale  __29__  fishing businesses with limited exports.\n\"Island  __30__  have been among the hardest hit by the threats facing the ocean,\" said President Tommy Remengesau Jr. in a statement. \"Creating this sanctuary is a bold move that the people of Palau recognise as  __31__  to our survival. We want to lead the way in restoring the health of the ocean for future generations.\"\nPalau has only been an  __32__  nation for twenty years and has a strong history of environmental protection. It is home to one of the world's finest marine ecosystems, with more than 1,300 species of fish and 700 species of coral.\nSenator Hokkons Baules, lead  __33__  of the Palau National Marine Sanctuary Act, said the sanctuary will \"help build a  __34__  future for the Palauan people by honoring the conservation traditions of our past\". These include the centuries-old custom of \"bul\", where leaders would call a temporary stop to fishing for key species in order to give fish  __35__  an opportunity to replenish(补充).", "matchIntro": "Data sharing: An open mind on open data", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "It is a movement building steady momentum: a call to make research data, software code and experimental methods publicly available and transparent. A spirit of openness is gaining acceptance in the science community, and is the only way, say advocates, to address a 'crisis' in science whereby too few findings are successfully reproduced. Furthermore, they say, it is the best way for researchers to gather the range of observations that are necessary to speed up discoveries or to identify large-scale trends."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The open-data shift poses a confusing problem for junior researchers. On the one hand, the drive to share is gathering official steam. Since 2013, global scientific bodies have begun to back policies that support increased public access to research. On the other hand, scientists disagree about how much and when they should share data, and they debate whether sharing it is more likely to accelerate science and make it more robust, or to introduce vulnerabilities and problems. As more journal and funders adopt data-sharing requirements, and as a growing number of enthusiasts call for more openness, junior researchers must find their place between adopters and those who continue to hold out, even as they strive to launch their own careers."}, {"label": "C", "text": "One key challenge facing young scientists is how to be open without becoming scientifically vulnerable. They must determine the risk of jeopardizing a job offer or a collaboration proposal from those who are wary of—or unfamiliar with—open science. And they must learn how to capitalize on the movement's benefits, such as opportunities for more citations and a way to build a reputation without the need for conventional metrics, such as publication in high-impact journals."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Some fields have embraced open data more than others. Researchers in psychology, a field rocked by findings of irreproducibility in the past few years, have been especially vocal supporters of the drive for more-open science. A few psychology journals have created incentives to increase interest in reproducible science—for example, by affixing an 'open-data' badge to articles that clearly state where data are available. According to social psychologist Brian Nosek, executive director of the Center for Open Science, the average data-sharing rate for the journal Psychological Science, which uses the badges, increased tenfold to 38% from 2013 to 2015."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Funders, too, are increasingly adopting an open-data policy. Several strongly encourage, and some require, a data-management plan that makes data available. The US National Science Foundation is among these. Some philanthropic(慈善的) funders, including the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation in Seattle, Washington, and the Wellcome Trust in London, also mandate open data from their grant recipients."}, {"label": "F", "text": "But many young researchers, especially those who have not been mentored in open science, are uncertain about whether to share or to stay private. Graduate students and postdocs, who often are working on their lab head's grant, may have no choice if their supervisor or another senior colleague opposes sharing."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Some fear that the potential impact of sharing is too high, especially at the early stages of a career. \"Everybody has a scary story about someone getting scooped(被抢先),\" says New York University astronomer David Hogg. Those fears may be a factor in a lingering hesitation to share data even when publishing in journals that mandate it."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Researchers at small labs or at institutions focused on teaching arguably have the most to lose when sharing hard-won data. \"With my institution and teaching load, I don't have postdocs and grad students,\" says Terry McGlynn, a tropical biologist at California State University, Dominguez Hills. \"The stakes are higher to share data because it's a bigger fraction of what's happening in my lab.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "Researchers also point to the time sink that is involved in preparing data for others to view. Once the data and associated materials appear in a repository(存储库), answering questions and handling complaints can take many hours."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The time investment can present other problems. In some cases, says data scientist Karthik Ram, it may be difficult for junior researchers to embrace openness when senior colleagues—many of whom head selection and promotion committees—might ridicule what they may view as misplaced energies. \"I've heard this recently—that embracing the idea of open data and code makes traditional academics uncomfortable,\" says Ram. \"The concern seems to be that open advocates don't spend their time being as productive as possible.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "An open-science stance can also add complexity to a collaboration. Kate Ratliff, who studies social attitudes at the University of Florida, Gainesville, says that it can seem as if there are two camps in a field—those who care about open science and those who don't. \"There's a new area to navigate—'Are you cool with the fact that I'll want to make the data open?'—when talking with somebody about an interesting research idea,\" she says."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Despite complications and concerns, the upsides of sharing can be significant. For example, when information is uploaded to a repository, a digital object identifier (DOI) is assigned. Scientists can use a DOI to publish each step of the research life cycle, not just the final paper. In so doing, they can potentially get three citations—one each for the data and software, in addition to the paper itself. And although some say that citations for software or data have little currency in academia, they can have other benefits."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Many advocates think that transparent data procedures with a data and time stamp will protect scientists from being scooped. \"This is the sweet spot between sharing and getting credit for it, while discouraging plagiarism(剽窃),\" says Ivo Grigorov, a project coordinator at the National Institute of Aquatic Resources Research Secretariat in Charlottenlund, Denmark. Hogg says that scooping is less of a problem than many think. \"The two cases I'm familiar with didn't involve open data or code,\" he says."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Open science also offers junior researchers the chance to level the playing field by gaining better access to crucial data. Ross Mounce, a postdoc studying evolutionary biology at the University of Cambridge, UK, is a vocal champion of open science, partly because his fossil-based research depends on access to others' data. He says that more openness in science could help to discourage what some perceive as a common practice of shutting out early-career scientists' requests for data."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Communication also helps for those who worry about jeopardizing a collaboration, he says. Concerns about open science should be discussed at the outset of a study. \"Whenever you start a project with someone, you have to establish a clear understanding of expectations for who owns the data, at what point they go public and who can do what with them,\" he says."}, {"label": "P", "text": "In the end, sharing data, software and materials with colleagues can help an early-career researcher to gain recognition—a crucial component of success. \"The thing you are searching for is reputation,\" says Titus Brown, a genomics(基因组学) researcher at the University of California, Davis. \"To get grants and jobs, you have to be relevant and achieve some level of public recognition. Anything you do that advances your presence—especially in a larger sphere, outside the communities you know—is a net win.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "In the beginning of the movie I, Robot, a robot has to decide whom to save after two cars plunge into the water—Del Spooner or a child. Even though Spooner screams \"Save her! Save her!\" the robot rescues him because it calculates that he has a 45 percent chance of survival compared to Sarah's 11 percent. The robot's decision and its calculated approach raise an important question: would humans make the same choice? And which choice would we want our robotic counterparts to make?\nIsaac Asimov evaded the whole notion of morality in devising his three laws of robotics, which hold that 1. Robots cannot harm humans or allow humans to come to harm; 2. Robots must obey humans, except where the order would conflict with law 1; and 3. Robots must act in self-preservation, unless doing so conflicts with laws 1 or 2. These laws are programmed into Asimov's robots—they don't have to think, judge, or value. They don't have to like humans or believe that hurting them is wrong or bad. They simply don't do it.\nThe robot who rescues Spooner's life in I, Robot follows Asimov's zeroth law: robots cannot harm humanity (as opposed to individual humans) or allow humanity to come to harm—an expansion of the first law that allows robots to determine what's in the greater good. Under the first law, a robot could not harm a dangerous gunman, but under the zeroth law, a robot could kill the gunman to save others.\nWhether it's possible to program a robot with safeguards such as Asimov's laws is debatable. A word such as \"harm\" is vague (what about emotional harm? Is replacing a human employee harm?), and abstract concepts present coding problems. The robots in Asimov's fiction expose complications and loopholes in the three laws, and even when the laws work, robots still have to assess situations.\nAssessing situations can be complicated. A robot has to identify the players, conditions, and possible outcomes for various scenarios. It's doubtful that a computer program can do that—at least, not without some undesirable results. A roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory programmed a robot to save human proxies(替身) called \"H-bots\" from danger. When one H-bot headed for danger, the robot successfully pushed it out of the way. But when two H-bots became imperiled, the robot choked 42 percent of the time, unable to decide which to save and letting them both \"die.\" The experiment highlights the importance of morality: without it, how can a robot decide whom to save or what's best for humanity, especially if it can't calculate survival odds?", "47": "In the beginning of the movie I, Robot, a robot has to decide whom to save after two cars plunge into the water—Del Spooner or a child. Even though Spooner screams \"Save her! Save her!\" the robot rescues him because it calculates that he has a 45 percent chance of survival compared to Sarah's 11 percent. The robot's decision and its calculated approach raise an important question: would humans make the same choice? And which choice would we want our robotic counterparts to make?\nIsaac Asimov evaded the whole notion of morality in devising his three laws of robotics, which hold that 1. Robots cannot harm humans or allow humans to come to harm; 2. Robots must obey humans, except where the order would conflict with law 1; and 3. Robots must act in self-preservation, unless doing so conflicts with laws 1 or 2. These laws are programmed into Asimov's robots—they don't have to think, judge, or value. They don't have to like humans or believe that hurting them is wrong or bad. They simply don't do it.\nThe robot who rescues Spooner's life in I, Robot follows Asimov's zeroth law: robots cannot harm humanity (as opposed to individual humans) or allow humanity to come to harm—an expansion of the first law that allows robots to determine what's in the greater good. Under the first law, a robot could not harm a dangerous gunman, but under the zeroth law, a robot could kill the gunman to save others.\nWhether it's possible to program a robot with safeguards such as Asimov's laws is debatable. A word such as \"harm\" is vague (what about emotional harm? Is replacing a human employee harm?), and abstract concepts present coding problems. The robots in Asimov's fiction expose complications and loopholes in the three laws, and even when the laws work, robots still have to assess situations.\nAssessing situations can be complicated. A robot has to identify the players, conditions, and possible outcomes for various scenarios. It's doubtful that a computer program can do that—at least, not without some undesirable results. A roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory programmed a robot to save human proxies(替身) called \"H-bots\" from danger. When one H-bot headed for danger, the robot successfully pushed it out of the way. But when two H-bots became imperiled, the robot choked 42 percent of the time, unable to decide which to save and letting them both \"die.\" The experiment highlights the importance of morality: without it, how can a robot decide whom to save or what's best for humanity, especially if it can't calculate survival odds?", "48": "In the beginning of the movie I, Robot, a robot has to decide whom to save after two cars plunge into the water—Del Spooner or a child. Even though Spooner screams \"Save her! Save her!\" the robot rescues him because it calculates that he has a 45 percent chance of survival compared to Sarah's 11 percent. The robot's decision and its calculated approach raise an important question: would humans make the same choice? And which choice would we want our robotic counterparts to make?\nIsaac Asimov evaded the whole notion of morality in devising his three laws of robotics, which hold that 1. Robots cannot harm humans or allow humans to come to harm; 2. Robots must obey humans, except where the order would conflict with law 1; and 3. Robots must act in self-preservation, unless doing so conflicts with laws 1 or 2. These laws are programmed into Asimov's robots—they don't have to think, judge, or value. They don't have to like humans or believe that hurting them is wrong or bad. They simply don't do it.\nThe robot who rescues Spooner's life in I, Robot follows Asimov's zeroth law: robots cannot harm humanity (as opposed to individual humans) or allow humanity to come to harm—an expansion of the first law that allows robots to determine what's in the greater good. Under the first law, a robot could not harm a dangerous gunman, but under the zeroth law, a robot could kill the gunman to save others.\nWhether it's possible to program a robot with safeguards such as Asimov's laws is debatable. A word such as \"harm\" is vague (what about emotional harm? Is replacing a human employee harm?), and abstract concepts present coding problems. The robots in Asimov's fiction expose complications and loopholes in the three laws, and even when the laws work, robots still have to assess situations.\nAssessing situations can be complicated. A robot has to identify the players, conditions, and possible outcomes for various scenarios. It's doubtful that a computer program can do that—at least, not without some undesirable results. A roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory programmed a robot to save human proxies(替身) called \"H-bots\" from danger. When one H-bot headed for danger, the robot successfully pushed it out of the way. But when two H-bots became imperiled, the robot choked 42 percent of the time, unable to decide which to save and letting them both \"die.\" The experiment highlights the importance of morality: without it, how can a robot decide whom to save or what's best for humanity, especially if it can't calculate survival odds?", "49": "In the beginning of the movie I, Robot, a robot has to decide whom to save after two cars plunge into the water—Del Spooner or a child. Even though Spooner screams \"Save her! Save her!\" the robot rescues him because it calculates that he has a 45 percent chance of survival compared to Sarah's 11 percent. The robot's decision and its calculated approach raise an important question: would humans make the same choice? And which choice would we want our robotic counterparts to make?\nIsaac Asimov evaded the whole notion of morality in devising his three laws of robotics, which hold that 1. Robots cannot harm humans or allow humans to come to harm; 2. Robots must obey humans, except where the order would conflict with law 1; and 3. Robots must act in self-preservation, unless doing so conflicts with laws 1 or 2. These laws are programmed into Asimov's robots—they don't have to think, judge, or value. They don't have to like humans or believe that hurting them is wrong or bad. They simply don't do it.\nThe robot who rescues Spooner's life in I, Robot follows Asimov's zeroth law: robots cannot harm humanity (as opposed to individual humans) or allow humanity to come to harm—an expansion of the first law that allows robots to determine what's in the greater good. Under the first law, a robot could not harm a dangerous gunman, but under the zeroth law, a robot could kill the gunman to save others.\nWhether it's possible to program a robot with safeguards such as Asimov's laws is debatable. A word such as \"harm\" is vague (what about emotional harm? Is replacing a human employee harm?), and abstract concepts present coding problems. The robots in Asimov's fiction expose complications and loopholes in the three laws, and even when the laws work, robots still have to assess situations.\nAssessing situations can be complicated. A robot has to identify the players, conditions, and possible outcomes for various scenarios. It's doubtful that a computer program can do that—at least, not without some undesirable results. A roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory programmed a robot to save human proxies(替身) called \"H-bots\" from danger. When one H-bot headed for danger, the robot successfully pushed it out of the way. But when two H-bots became imperiled, the robot choked 42 percent of the time, unable to decide which to save and letting them both \"die.\" The experiment highlights the importance of morality: without it, how can a robot decide whom to save or what's best for humanity, especially if it can't calculate survival odds?", "50": "In the beginning of the movie I, Robot, a robot has to decide whom to save after two cars plunge into the water—Del Spooner or a child. Even though Spooner screams \"Save her! Save her!\" the robot rescues him because it calculates that he has a 45 percent chance of survival compared to Sarah's 11 percent. The robot's decision and its calculated approach raise an important question: would humans make the same choice? And which choice would we want our robotic counterparts to make?\nIsaac Asimov evaded the whole notion of morality in devising his three laws of robotics, which hold that 1. Robots cannot harm humans or allow humans to come to harm; 2. Robots must obey humans, except where the order would conflict with law 1; and 3. Robots must act in self-preservation, unless doing so conflicts with laws 1 or 2. These laws are programmed into Asimov's robots—they don't have to think, judge, or value. They don't have to like humans or believe that hurting them is wrong or bad. They simply don't do it.\nThe robot who rescues Spooner's life in I, Robot follows Asimov's zeroth law: robots cannot harm humanity (as opposed to individual humans) or allow humanity to come to harm—an expansion of the first law that allows robots to determine what's in the greater good. Under the first law, a robot could not harm a dangerous gunman, but under the zeroth law, a robot could kill the gunman to save others.\nWhether it's possible to program a robot with safeguards such as Asimov's laws is debatable. A word such as \"harm\" is vague (what about emotional harm? Is replacing a human employee harm?), and abstract concepts present coding problems. The robots in Asimov's fiction expose complications and loopholes in the three laws, and even when the laws work, robots still have to assess situations.\nAssessing situations can be complicated. A robot has to identify the players, conditions, and possible outcomes for various scenarios. It's doubtful that a computer program can do that—at least, not without some undesirable results. A roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory programmed a robot to save human proxies(替身) called \"H-bots\" from danger. When one H-bot headed for danger, the robot successfully pushed it out of the way. But when two H-bots became imperiled, the robot choked 42 percent of the time, unable to decide which to save and letting them both \"die.\" The experiment highlights the importance of morality: without it, how can a robot decide whom to save or what's best for humanity, especially if it can't calculate survival odds?", "51": "Our world now moves so fast that we seldom stop to see just how far we have come in just a few years. The latest iPhone 6s, for example, has a dual-core processor and fits nicely into your pocket. By comparison, you would expect to find a technological specification like this on your standard laptop in an office anywhere in the world.\nIt's no wonder that new applications for the Internet of Things are moving ahead fast when almost every new device we buy has a plug on the end of it or a wireless connection to the internet. Soon, our current smartphone lifestyle will expand to create our own smart home lifestyle too.\nAll researchers agree that close to 25 billion devices, things and sensors will be connected by 2020 which incidentally is also the moment that Millennials(千禧一代) are expected to make up 75 percent of our overall workforce, and the fully connected home will become a reality for large numbers of people worldwide.\nHowever, this is just the tip of the proverbial iceberg as smart buildings and even cities increasingly become the norm as leaders and business owners begin to wake up to the massive savings that technology can deliver through connected sensors and new forms of automation coupled with intelligent energy and facilities management.\nOnline security cameras, intelligent lighting and a wealth of sensors that control both temperature and air quality are offering an unprecedented level of control, efficiency, and improvements to what were once classed necessary costs when running a business or managing a large building.\nWe can expect that the ever-growing list of devices, systems and environments remains connected, always online and talking to each other. The big benefit will not only be in the housing of this enormous and rapidly growing amount of data, but will also be in the ability to run real time data analytics to extract actionable and ongoing knowledge.\nThe biggest and most exciting challenge of this technology is how to creatively leverage this ever-growing amount of data to deliver cost savings, improvements and tangible benefits to both businesses and citizens of these smart cities.\nThe good news is that most of this technology is already invented. Let's face it, it wasn't too long ago that the idea of working from anywhere and at anytime was some form of a distant Utopian(乌托邦式的) dream, and yet now we can perform almost any office-based task from any location in the world as long as we have access to the internet.\nIt's time to wake up to the fact that making smart buildings, cities and homes will dramatically improve our quality of life in the years ahead.", "52": "Our world now moves so fast that we seldom stop to see just how far we have come in just a few years. The latest iPhone 6s, for example, has a dual-core processor and fits nicely into your pocket. By comparison, you would expect to find a technological specification like this on your standard laptop in an office anywhere in the world.\nIt's no wonder that new applications for the Internet of Things are moving ahead fast when almost every new device we buy has a plug on the end of it or a wireless connection to the internet. Soon, our current smartphone lifestyle will expand to create our own smart home lifestyle too.\nAll researchers agree that close to 25 billion devices, things and sensors will be connected by 2020 which incidentally is also the moment that Millennials(千禧一代) are expected to make up 75 percent of our overall workforce, and the fully connected home will become a reality for large numbers of people worldwide.\nHowever, this is just the tip of the proverbial iceberg as smart buildings and even cities increasingly become the norm as leaders and business owners begin to wake up to the massive savings that technology can deliver through connected sensors and new forms of automation coupled with intelligent energy and facilities management.\nOnline security cameras, intelligent lighting and a wealth of sensors that control both temperature and air quality are offering an unprecedented level of control, efficiency, and improvements to what were once classed necessary costs when running a business or managing a large building.\nWe can expect that the ever-growing list of devices, systems and environments remains connected, always online and talking to each other. The big benefit will not only be in the housing of this enormous and rapidly growing amount of data, but will also be in the ability to run real time data analytics to extract actionable and ongoing knowledge.\nThe biggest and most exciting challenge of this technology is how to creatively leverage this ever-growing amount of data to deliver cost savings, improvements and tangible benefits to both businesses and citizens of these smart cities.\nThe good news is that most of this technology is already invented. Let's face it, it wasn't too long ago that the idea of working from anywhere and at anytime was some form of a distant Utopian(乌托邦式的) dream, and yet now we can perform almost any office-based task from any location in the world as long as we have access to the internet.\nIt's time to wake up to the fact that making smart buildings, cities and homes will dramatically improve our quality of life in the years ahead.", "53": "Our world now moves so fast that we seldom stop to see just how far we have come in just a few years. The latest iPhone 6s, for example, has a dual-core processor and fits nicely into your pocket. By comparison, you would expect to find a technological specification like this on your standard laptop in an office anywhere in the world.\nIt's no wonder that new applications for the Internet of Things are moving ahead fast when almost every new device we buy has a plug on the end of it or a wireless connection to the internet. Soon, our current smartphone lifestyle will expand to create our own smart home lifestyle too.\nAll researchers agree that close to 25 billion devices, things and sensors will be connected by 2020 which incidentally is also the moment that Millennials(千禧一代) are expected to make up 75 percent of our overall workforce, and the fully connected home will become a reality for large numbers of people worldwide.\nHowever, this is just the tip of the proverbial iceberg as smart buildings and even cities increasingly become the norm as leaders and business owners begin to wake up to the massive savings that technology can deliver through connected sensors and new forms of automation coupled with intelligent energy and facilities management.\nOnline security cameras, intelligent lighting and a wealth of sensors that control both temperature and air quality are offering an unprecedented level of control, efficiency, and improvements to what were once classed necessary costs when running a business or managing a large building.\nWe can expect that the ever-growing list of devices, systems and environments remains connected, always online and talking to each other. The big benefit will not only be in the housing of this enormous and rapidly growing amount of data, but will also be in the ability to run real time data analytics to extract actionable and ongoing knowledge.\nThe biggest and most exciting challenge of this technology is how to creatively leverage this ever-growing amount of data to deliver cost savings, improvements and tangible benefits to both businesses and citizens of these smart cities.\nThe good news is that most of this technology is already invented. Let's face it, it wasn't too long ago that the idea of working from anywhere and at anytime was some form of a distant Utopian(乌托邦式的) dream, and yet now we can perform almost any office-based task from any location in the world as long as we have access to the internet.\nIt's time to wake up to the fact that making smart buildings, cities and homes will dramatically improve our quality of life in the years ahead.", "54": "Our world now moves so fast that we seldom stop to see just how far we have come in just a few years. The latest iPhone 6s, for example, has a dual-core processor and fits nicely into your pocket. By comparison, you would expect to find a technological specification like this on your standard laptop in an office anywhere in the world.\nIt's no wonder that new applications for the Internet of Things are moving ahead fast when almost every new device we buy has a plug on the end of it or a wireless connection to the internet. Soon, our current smartphone lifestyle will expand to create our own smart home lifestyle too.\nAll researchers agree that close to 25 billion devices, things and sensors will be connected by 2020 which incidentally is also the moment that Millennials(千禧一代) are expected to make up 75 percent of our overall workforce, and the fully connected home will become a reality for large numbers of people worldwide.\nHowever, this is just the tip of the proverbial iceberg as smart buildings and even cities increasingly become the norm as leaders and business owners begin to wake up to the massive savings that technology can deliver through connected sensors and new forms of automation coupled with intelligent energy and facilities management.\nOnline security cameras, intelligent lighting and a wealth of sensors that control both temperature and air quality are offering an unprecedented level of control, efficiency, and improvements to what were once classed necessary costs when running a business or managing a large building.\nWe can expect that the ever-growing list of devices, systems and environments remains connected, always online and talking to each other. The big benefit will not only be in the housing of this enormous and rapidly growing amount of data, but will also be in the ability to run real time data analytics to extract actionable and ongoing knowledge.\nThe biggest and most exciting challenge of this technology is how to creatively leverage this ever-growing amount of data to deliver cost savings, improvements and tangible benefits to both businesses and citizens of these smart cities.\nThe good news is that most of this technology is already invented. Let's face it, it wasn't too long ago that the idea of working from anywhere and at anytime was some form of a distant Utopian(乌托邦式的) dream, and yet now we can perform almost any office-based task from any location in the world as long as we have access to the internet.\nIt's time to wake up to the fact that making smart buildings, cities and homes will dramatically improve our quality of life in the years ahead.", "55": "Our world now moves so fast that we seldom stop to see just how far we have come in just a few years. The latest iPhone 6s, for example, has a dual-core processor and fits nicely into your pocket. By comparison, you would expect to find a technological specification like this on your standard laptop in an office anywhere in the world.\nIt's no wonder that new applications for the Internet of Things are moving ahead fast when almost every new device we buy has a plug on the end of it or a wireless connection to the internet. Soon, our current smartphone lifestyle will expand to create our own smart home lifestyle too.\nAll researchers agree that close to 25 billion devices, things and sensors will be connected by 2020 which incidentally is also the moment that Millennials(千禧一代) are expected to make up 75 percent of our overall workforce, and the fully connected home will become a reality for large numbers of people worldwide.\nHowever, this is just the tip of the proverbial iceberg as smart buildings and even cities increasingly become the norm as leaders and business owners begin to wake up to the massive savings that technology can deliver through connected sensors and new forms of automation coupled with intelligent energy and facilities management.\nOnline security cameras, intelligent lighting and a wealth of sensors that control both temperature and air quality are offering an unprecedented level of control, efficiency, and improvements to what were once classed necessary costs when running a business or managing a large building.\nWe can expect that the ever-growing list of devices, systems and environments remains connected, always online and talking to each other. The big benefit will not only be in the housing of this enormous and rapidly growing amount of data, but will also be in the ability to run real time data analytics to extract actionable and ongoing knowledge.\nThe biggest and most exciting challenge of this technology is how to creatively leverage this ever-growing amount of data to deliver cost savings, improvements and tangible benefits to both businesses and citizens of these smart cities.\nThe good news is that most of this technology is already invented. Let's face it, it wasn't too long ago that the idea of working from anywhere and at anytime was some form of a distant Utopian(乌托邦式的) dream, and yet now we can perform almost any office-based task from any location in the world as long as we have access to the internet.\nIt's time to wake up to the fact that making smart buildings, cities and homes will dramatically improve our quality of life in the years ahead."}, "questions": [{"id": "2017-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What is the man asked to do this evening?", "options": ["A) Say a few words to thank the speaker.", "B) Introduce the speaker to the audience.", "C) Give a lecture on the history of the town.", "D) Host a talk on how to give a good speech."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Say a few words to thank the speaker.\n【定位】You are going to give a short speech of thanks for the speaker this evening, aren't you, Bill?\n你今晚要为发言人做一个简短的致谢发言，对吧，Bill？\n【信号】对话开篇的直接交代+男方确认。答案句是女方开场的交代 give a short speech of thanks\nfor the speaker this evening，紧接男方在 Yes. 中确认，'一问一答得到肯定'就是答案\n信号。\n【替换】give a short speech of thanks for the speaker ↔ Say a few words to thank the speaker\n致谢发言=说几句感谢的话\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nIntroduce the speaker to the audience. 借用了录音里反复出现的 speaker 一词，但男方\n是要'感谢'发言人，不是'介绍'发言人，动作完全不同，听到 speaker 就选 B 会中招。\n借用原文：speech of thanks for the speaker\nC) 张冠李戴\nGive a lecture on the history of the town. 拼接了录音后文的 our town in the mid-18th\ncentury 等历史话题词，但讲本镇历史的是 Miss Bligh，不是男方今晚的任务，属张冠李戴\n式的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：our town in the mid-18th century\nD) 未提及\nHost a talk on how to give a good speech. 用了 talk、speech 这些原词，但录音从没说\n男方要'主持一场关于如何演讲的讲座'，是凭原词无中生有。\n借用原文：short speech of thanks", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do we learn about the man?", "options": ["A) He was the founder of the local history society.", "B) He has worked with Miss Bligh for 20 years.", "C) He has published a book on public speaking.", "D) He joined the local history society when young."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He joined the local history society when young.\n【定位】Well, I was in my early twenties. I joined the local history society.\n嗯，那时我二十出头。我加入了本地历史协会。\n【信号】被问者回忆往事时的自述动作句。答案句是男方回忆经历时的自述 I was in my early\ntwenties. I joined the local history society.，年龄状语 in my early twenties 与动作\njoined 一并出现，是承载身份信息的关键句。\n【替换】I was in my early twenties. I joined the local history society. ↔\nHe joined the local history society when young\n二十出头=年轻时\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHe was the founder of the local history society. 用了原词 local history society，但录\n音说他是 joined（加入），不是 founder（创办），把'加入'偷换成'创办'，听到组织名就\n选会错。\n借用原文：I joined the local history society\nB) 未提及\nHe has worked with Miss Bligh for 20 years. 用了后文出现的 Miss Bligh 这个人名，并\n凭空造出 20 年共事；录音里他与 Miss Bligh 只是一次讲座上的偶遇，从无共事，是数字\n+人名的双重原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a lecture by a Miss Bligh\nC) 张冠李戴\nHe has published a book on public speaking. 借用了 She'd just published a book，但\n出书的是 Miss Bligh、且写的是本镇历史不是演讲，把她的事安到男方头上，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：She'd just published a book on the subject", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the man say about Miss Bligh?", "options": ["A) She was obviously better at talking than writing.", "B) She had a good knowledge of the town's history.", "C) Her speech was so funny as to amuse the audience.", "D) Her ancestors came to the town in the 18th century."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She had a good knowledge of the town's history.\n【定位】She'd just published a book on the subject which was reckoned to be quite good.\n她刚就这个主题出了一本书，这本书被认为相当不错。\n【信号】谈及该人时的事实陈述句。答案句是男方介绍 Miss Bligh 时的事实 She was going to talk\nwith slides about our town in the mid-18th century. She'd just published a book on\nthe subject——她讲本镇历史、且就此出过书，正面陈述她在本镇历史上的造诣。\n【替换】talk with slides about our town in the mid-18th century ↔\nhad a good knowledge of the town's history\n讲本镇18世纪+出书=懂本镇历史\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nShe was obviously better at talking than writing. 暗示她口才胜过写作，但录音说她 so\nnervous that she kept forgetting what to say, and she spoke almost in a whisper\n——讲得磕磕巴巴、声音小到听不见，恰恰说明她不擅长talking，与选项相反。\n借用原文：she kept forgetting what to say, and she spoke almost in a whisper\nC) 偷换概念\nHer speech was so funny as to amuse the audience. 套用了录音的尴尬氛围，但 It was\nembarrassing. 说的是令人尴尬、不是逗乐观众，funny（搞笑）与 embarrassing（尴\n尬）含义相反。\n借用原文：It was embarrassing.\nD) 原词陷阱\nHer ancestors came to the town in the 18th century. 借了录音里真念过的 mid-18th\ncentury，但那个世纪是她讲座的'主题年代'，不是她祖先迁来的时间，录音从没提她的\nancestors，是典型数字原词陷阱。\n借用原文：our town in the mid-18th century", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say about the first time he gave a speech of thanks?", "options": ["A) He read exactly what was written in his notes.", "B) He kept forgetting what he was going to say.", "C) He made an embarrassing remark.", "D) He was too nervous to speak up"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He made an embarrassing remark.\n【定位】I mentioned the interesting facts, referred to the excellent slides, and then finished up by\nsaying \"We'd all like to thank Miss Bligh for blowing us her slides.\"\n我提到了那些有趣的事实，谈到了精彩的幻灯片，最后以一句\"我们都想感谢 Miss Bligh 为\n我们炸掉了她的幻灯片\"作结。\n【信号】他自述致谢词时的口误+随后认错。答案句是他复述自己致谢词 We'd all like to thank\nMiss Bligh for blowing us her slides.（把 showing 说成 blowing），紧接 I felt terrible.\nI tried to apologize——'说完就觉得糟糕、要道歉'这一反应是认定他说错话的信号。\n【替换】thank Miss Bligh for blowing us her slides ↔ He made an embarrassing remark\n口误说成blowing=令人尴尬的话\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nHe read exactly what was written in his notes. 拼了录音里的 notes、read，但 I'll write\ndown exactly what I'm going to say and read it carefully. 说的是'今晚'要照稿念，不\n是'第一次'；第一次他只是 make notes during the lecture 记笔记，并未照稿念，把今晚\n的做法错安到第一次。\n借用原文：I'll write down exactly what I'm going to say and read it carefully.\nB) 张冠李戴\nHe kept forgetting what he was going to say. 借用 she kept forgetting what to say，\n但忘词的是 Miss Bligh（She），不是男方（He），把她的窘况安到他头上，是典型张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：she kept forgetting what to say\nD) 张冠李戴\nHe was too nervous to speak up 借了 she was so nervous 与 spoke almost in a\nwhisper，但'太紧张、声音小到说不响'的是 Miss Bligh，男方反而 by the time Miss Bligh\nstood up to talk, I was feeling much better（轻松多了），与选项相反。\n借用原文：she was so nervous that she kept forgetting what to say, and she spoke almost in\na whisper", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman think the man should know?", "options": ["A) What their retailers demand.", "B) What their rivals are doing.", "C) How they are going to beat their rivals.", "D) How dramatically the market is changing."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） What their rivals are doing.\n【定位】W: Well, our rivals are offering extended credit terms to some of the retailers in the area.\n女：嗯，我们的竞争对手正在向本地区的一些零售商提供延长的信用付款期限。\n【信号】提醒后紧跟的内容句。前面她说 there is something you should know about（有件事你\n该知道），男方追问 What's that?，她紧接着给出的这句 our rivals are offering\nextended credit terms 就是被点名的 \"那件事\"，应答即答案区。\n【替换】our rivals are offering extended credit terms to some of the retailers ↔\nWhat their rivals are doing.\n具体动作概括为正在做的事\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实念到 retailers，A 也写 What their retailers demand.，听到 retailers 就想选；\n但原文是 \"对手向零售商提供\"，零售商是被动接受方，根本没说 \"零售商要求什么\"。\n借用原文：retailers in the area\nC) 过度推断\n她只是告知对手在做什么，并未谈 \"我们要如何打败对手\"。C 的 How they are going to\nbeat their rivals. 借了 rivals 一词，却把 \"通报情况\" 过度推断成 \"反击方案\"，原文没有。\n借用原文：our rivals\nD) 与原文相反\n对话开头她明说 Nothing dramatic.（没什么大事），D 却说 How dramatically the\nmarket is changing.，盗用 dramatic 一词却反了向，正是 \"市场没有剧变\"。\n借用原文：Nothing dramatic.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman think of Barratts Company?", "options": ["A) They should be taken seriously.", "B) They are rapidly catching up.", "C) Their business strategy is quite effective.", "D) Their potential has been underestimated."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They should be taken seriously.\n【定位】W: I know they are smaller than us, but we can't afford to ignore them.\n女：我知道他们比我们小，但我们承受不起忽视他们的后果。\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真态度。前半 I know they are smaller than us 是让步铺垫，转折词 but\n后的 we can't afford to ignore them 才是她真正的立场——不能忽视、得当回事。态度题\n的落点几乎都在 but 之后。\n【替换】we can't afford to ignore them ↔ They should be taken seriously.\n否定的否定等于肯定主张\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 说 They are rapidly catching up.（在快速追赶），原文只说对手 smaller than us，从\n未说他们在追赶逼近，属凭空添加的情节。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说对手 Their business strategy is quite effective.，但她全程没评价对手 \"策略是否有\n效\"，只说 \"不能忽视\"。这是把 \"该重视\" 偷换成另一维度 \"策略有效\"。\nD) 过度推断\nD 说 Their potential has been underestimated.（潜力被低估），听感上和 \"该重视\" 很\n近，易被诱选；但原文谈的是 \"现在不能忽视\"，并未谈 \"潜力被低估\"，是过度引申。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did the woman say about the letter from the tax revenue office?", "options": ["A) She had given it to Tom.", "B) It simply made her go frantic.", "C) She had not seen it yet.", "D) It was not much of a big concern."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She had not seen it yet.\n【定位】W: No. And he was away before I could get hold of the letter.\n女：没有。而且我还没来得及拿到那封信，他就已经离开了。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。男方问 Did he find them?，她答 No，并补充关键一句 he was away\nbefore I could get hold of the letter——她自己 \"还没拿到 / 还没看到那封信\"。考点正落\n在被问者这句应答里。\n【替换】he was away before I could get hold of the letter ↔ She had not seen it yet.\n没拿到等于没看到\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文确有人名 Tom got this letter，A 写 She had given it to Tom. 借了 Tom，听到就想\n选；但原文是 \"Tom 自己收到了信\"，不是 \"她把信给了 Tom\"，把收信人安成了受赠人。\n借用原文：Tom got this letter\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文 Tom got this letter late yesterday and then went frantic，抓狂（frantic）的是\nTom；B 写 It simply made her go frantic. 盗用 frantic，却把抓狂者换成 \"她\"。\n借用原文：Tom got this letter late yesterday and then went frantic\nD) 与原文相反\nTom 收到信后 went frantic、又找账目未果，氛围明显是当回事；D 却说 It was not much\nof a big concern.（不算大事），与原文相反，且原文没人这样说过。\n借用原文：went frantic", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What is the man thinking of doing?", "options": ["A) Restructuring the whole company.", "B) Employing more forwarding agents.", "C) Promoting cooperation with Jayal Motors.", "D) Exporting their motorbikes to Indonesia."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Exporting their motorbikes to Indonesia.\n【定位】M: How about selling that new motorcycle of yours in Indonesia? — W: What? You\nmean export?\n男：把你那款新摩托车卖到印度尼西亚去怎么样？——女：什么？你是说出口？\n【信号】提议句 How about + 对方确认。男方用提议句 How about selling that new motorcycle\nof yours in Indonesia? 抛出计划，女方随即确认 You mean export?，一问一答把这件\n\"打算做的事\" 钉死为 \"出口到印尼\"。\n【替换】selling that new motorcycle of yours in Indonesia ↔ Exporting their motorbikes to Indonesia.\n卖到外国即出口\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nWe'd have to reorganize the whole company. 是女方提出的顾虑，男方随即反驳 Don't\nbe silly. I don't intend starting next week.。A 的 Restructuring the whole company.\n盗用 reorganize，却既非男方打算、又被他否掉。\n借用原文：We'd have to reorganize the whole company.\nB) 原词陷阱\nforwarding agents 出现在 That's what forwarding agents are for.，那是回应运输难题\n的一句解释，不是他打算 \"雇更多代理\"。B 的 Employing more forwarding agents. 盗用\nforwarding agents 却答非所问。\n借用原文：That's what forwarding agents are for.\nC) 原词陷阱\nJayal Motors 只是他们自家公司名 This is Jayal Motors.，原文从无 \"与 Jayal Motors 合\n作\" 之意。C 的 Promoting cooperation with Jayal Motors. 借专名虚构了一个合作计\n划。\n借用原文：This is Jayal Motors.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about the report released this month?", "options": ["A) It makes claims in conflict with the existing research.", "B) It focuses on the link between bedtime and nutrition.", "C) It cautions against the overuse of coffee and alcohol.", "D) It shows that \"night owls\" work much less efficiently."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It focuses on the link between bedtime and nutrition.\n【定位】A report on sleep and nutrition released this month found that people who consistently went\nto bed earlier than 11 p.m. took in fewer calories and ate more healthy food.\n本月发布的一份关于睡眠与营养的报道发现：那些一贯在晚上11点前就寝的人，摄入的卡路\n里更少、吃的健康食物更多。\n【信号】报道开篇的自我定位句。录音第一句就交代了报道的主题与发现：A report on sleep and\nnutrition released this month found that…。篇章里“某报道/研究发现……”的开篇句，就\n是它对自身“在讲什么”的定位，是问报道内容时的天然答案句。\n【替换】A report on sleep and nutrition ↔ the link between bedtime and nutrition\n睡眠饮食 = 就寝与营养的联系\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说报道“与现有研究冲突”，但第二段明说 This report corresponds with the existing\nscientific literature on bedtime and wellness.——corresponds with 是“吻合/一致”，与\nA 的 in conflict with（冲突）正好相反。A 盗用了“existing research/literature”这个原\n词，却把方向反了。\n借用原文：This report corresponds with the existing scientific literature\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说报道“告诫不要过量喝咖啡和酒”，录音确实念到 consume more coffee, alcohol,\nrefined sugars and processed meats——但那只是描述“夜猫子吃喝什么”的举例，报道并\n没有“caution against（告诫/警告）”过量使用。C 盗用了 coffee/alcohol 这两个具体原词\n来诱你“听到就选”。\n借用原文：consume more coffee, alcohol, refined sugars and processed meats\nD) 未提及\nD 说报道显示“夜猫子工作效率低得多”，但全文只谈“夜猫子”的饮食、卡路里与体重，从未\n提及 work efficiently（工作效率）。D 借用了 night owls 这个原词，却安上录音根本没出\n现的“效率”话题。\n借用原文：“night owls” who go to bed between 11 p.m. and 3 a.m.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the study from the University of California, Berkeley, find about teens who go to bed late?", "options": ["A) They pay greater attention to food choice.", "B) They tend to achieve less than their peers.", "C) They run a higher risk of gaining weight.", "D) They stand a greater chance to fall sick."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They run a higher risk of gaining weight.\n【定位】And a 2015 study from the University of California, Berkeley found that teens who go to bed\nlate are more likely to gain weight over a five-year period.\n还有一项2015年来自加州大学伯克利分校的研究发现：晚睡的青少年在五年间更有可能\n长胖。\n【信号】专名锚点+found that 的发现句。录音里出现题干同款专名 a 2015 study from the\nUniversity of California, Berkeley found that…，紧跟的 found that 后面就是该研究的发\n现。专名是听力里最硬的定位锚，听到它就锁定后半句。\n【替换】are more likely to gain weight ↔ run a higher risk of gaining weight\n更可能长胖 = 长胖风险更高\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nA 说“他们更注意食物选择”，原文里与 food choice 相关的是另一项研究 people who\nsleep more tend to eat less unhealthy food，且方向是“睡得多的人吃得更健康”，与“晚\n睡青少年”无关。A 把别处的内容安到了伯克利研究头上。\n借用原文：people who sleep more tend to eat less unhealthy food\nB) 未提及\nB 说他们“比同龄人成就更低”，原文谈晚睡青少年只说会 gain weight，从未提及 achieve\n（成就）。B 里的 their peers（同龄人）确实在原文出现过（their peers who don't get\nas much rest），但“成就更低”是凭空安的。\n借用原文：their peers who don't get as much rest\nD) 未提及\nD 说他们“更容易生病”，但伯克利研究只说更可能 gain weight（长胖），全篇没提 fall\nsick（生病）。D 是把“不健康”按常识引申成了“生病”，原文并无此话。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What should \"night owls\" do to reduce their consumption of unhealthy food?", "options": ["A) Get up late.", "B) Sleep 8 hours a day.", "C) Exercise more.", "D) Go to bed earlier"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Go to bed earlier\n【定位】Still, if late sleepers want to lose a few pounds, they can go to bed earlier than they usually\ndo, thereby reducing their chances of taking snacks before bedtime.\n不过，晚睡者如果想减掉几磅，可以比平时更早上床睡觉，从而减少睡前吃零食的机会。\n【信号】if…they can 的建议句。录音结尾用条件+建议结构 if late sleepers want to…, they can\ngo to bed earlier，they can… 正是给出的具体做法。这种“如果想……他们可以……”就是篇\n章里典型的建议信号，是问“该怎么做”的答案句。\n【替换】they can go to bed earlier than they usually do ↔ Go to bed earlier\n几乎原句保留的建议动作\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说“晚起”，与录音建议的 go to bed earlier（早睡）方向相反。A 利用 get up/bed 这类\n“作息”原词制造混淆，但极性反了。\n借用原文：they can go to bed earlier than they usually do\nB) 未提及\nB 带具体数字“每天睡8小时”，听着精确，但录音建议是“比平时更早睡”，从没出现 8 hours\n这个量。B 是凭“睡够觉”的常识硬安了一个数字。\nC) 未提及\nC 说“多运动”，按“减肥/健康”的生活常识很顺，但录音针对“减少不健康饮食”给的唯一做法\n是早睡，全篇未提 exercise。C 是常识替了文本。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why has the Oscars ceremony been criticized?", "options": ["A) All of the acting nominees are white.", "B) It has got too much publicity on TV.", "C) It is prejudiced against foreign films.", "D) Only 7% of the nominees are female."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） All of the acting nominees are white.\n【定位】With less than a week before an Oscars ceremony that has already been criticized for an all-\nwhite list of acting nominees, a study shows the film industry does worse than television.\n在距奥斯卡颁奖典礼不到一周之际——该典礼已因一份全是白人的演员提名名单而饱受批评\n——一项研究显示，电影业的表现比电视业更差。\n【信号】评判信号 criticized for。句中 criticized for an all-white list of acting nominees 用\ncriticized for 直接点出\"因……而被批评\"，for 后面正是被批评的原因，这是定位原因题答\n案句最硬的信号。\n【替换】an all-white list of acting nominees ↔ All of the acting nominees are white.\n名词短语改写成完整句\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nIt has got too much publicity on TV. 借了原文反复出现的 TV/television 一词，但原文谈\n的是电影业比电视业\"表现更差\"，从未说典礼\"上电视宣传太多\"，属听到 TV 就想选的原词\n陷阱。\n借用原文：the film industry does worse than television\nC) 未提及\nIt is prejudiced against foreign films. 说的\"歧视外国电影\"全篇没有任何对应，原文批的\n是族裔/性别代表性不足，与外国片无关，属无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nOnly 7% of the nominees are female. 盗用了原文的数字 7%，但原文 only 7 percent of\nfilms had a cast whose balance of race and ethnicity reflected the country's diversity\n里 7% 修饰的是\"族裔均衡的影片\"，且这是研究数据、不是典礼被批的理由，数字与对象都\n被错配。\n借用原文：only 7 percent of films had a cast whose balance of race and ethnicity reflected the\ncountry's diversity", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do we learn from the research?", "options": ["A) 22 percent of movie directors were people of color.", "B) Half of the TV programs were ethnically balanced.", "C) Only one-fifth of TV shows had black characters.", "D) Only 3.4 percent of film directors were women."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Only 3.4 percent of film directors were women.\n【定位】Just 3.4 percent of film directors were female, and only 7 percent of films had a cast whose\nbalance of race and ethnicity reflected the country's diversity.\n仅有 3.4% 的电影导演是女性，只有 7% 的影片其演员阵容在种族与族裔比例上反映了这个\n国家的多样性。\n【信号】数字+群体的直陈句。句首 Just 3.4 percent of film directors were female 把数字", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "Who are regarded as \"largely invisible\" on TV shows?", "options": ["A) Non-white males.", "B) Program creators.", "C) Females of color over 40.", "D) Asian speaking characters."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Females of color over 40.\n【定位】When researchers looked at all TV shows, they also found that women of color over 40 were\nregarded as \"largely invisible\".\n当研究者考察所有电视节目时，他们还发现 40 岁以上的有色人种女性被视为\"基本隐形\"。\n【信号】引号原话精确命中。原文 women of color over 40 were regarded as \"largely invisible\"\n里 largely invisible 与题干引号原话逐字相同，这种引号短语是听力里最强的定位信号，紧\n挨它的人群即答案。\n【替换】women of color over 40 ↔ Females of color over 40.\nwomen≈Females 余皆同\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nNon-white males. 的\"非白人男性\"与原文被视为隐形的 women of color over 40（有色女\n性）在性别上正相反，原文从未把 invisible 安在男性身上。\n借用原文：women of color over 40 were regarded as \"largely invisible\"\nB) 张冠李戴\nProgram creators. 借了同段出现的 creators 一词，但原文 just 22 percent of TV series\ncreators were female 里 creators 是另一项数据的主角，被视为隐形的并非创作者，属把\n别处的词安到本题。\n借用原文：just 22 percent of TV series creators were female\nD) 原词陷阱\nAsian speaking characters. 搬来原文 no Asian speaking characters 的原词，但那是第 4\n段讲影视缺亚裔台词角色的数据，与第 3 段\"隐形\"所指的有色女性不是一回事。\n借用原文：half the films and TV shows had no Asian speaking characters", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the speaker say about Hispanics?", "options": ["A) They constitute 17% of Hollywood movie characters.", "B) They are most underrepresented across TV and film.", "C) They contribute little to the U. S. film industry.", "D) They account for 8.5% of the U.S. population"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are most underrepresented across TV and film.\n【定位】Across TV and film, the under-representation of non-white characters falls mostly\non Hispanics.\n在电视与电影中，非白人角色的代表性不足问题最主要地落在西班牙裔身上。\n【信号】程度词 mostly 定最甚者。原文 the under-representation of non-white characters falls\nmostly on Hispanics 用 mostly（最主要地）把\"代表性最不足\"的帽子扣给西班牙裔，\nmostly 这种程度词正是答案信号，对应选项里的最高级 most。\n【替换】the under-representation of non-white characters falls mostly on Hispanics ↔\nThey are most underrepresented across TV and film.\n名词化转句、mostly≈most\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey constitute 17% of Hollywood movie characters. 盗用原文数字 17%，但原文\ndespite being about 17 percent of the U.S. population 里 17% 指的是西班牙裔占\"美国\n人口\"的比例，不是占\"好莱坞电影角色\"的比例，恰恰他们只占角色的 5.8%，数字对、对象\n错。\n借用原文：despite being about 17 percent of the U.S. population\nC) 过度推断\nThey contribute little to the U. S. film industry. 说西班牙裔\"对电影业贡献小\"，原文只讲\n他们\"代表性不足\"、并未评价其贡献，是把\"出镜少\"过度推断成\"贡献少\"。\nD) 未提及\nThey account for 8.5% of the U.S. population 给的 8.5% 在原文里根本没出现——原文\n说他们占人口约 about 17 percent of the U.S. population、占角色 5.8%，8.5% 是凭空\n捏造的数字。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What life insurance should a single person buy according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) One that can provide for emergency needs.", "B) One that can pay for their medical expenses.", "C) One that covers their debts and burial expenses", "D) One that ensures a healthy life for their later years."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） One that covers their debts and burial expenses\n【定位】Jerry is single and has no dependents. Probably the only life insurance he needs is enough to\ncover his debts and burial expenses.\n杰瑞单身，没有需要供养的人。他大概唯一需要的人寿保险，就是足以支付其债务和丧葬费\n用的那种。\n【信号】以\"单身无人供养\"点出对象后给出需求。录音先用 Jerry is single and has no dependents\n点出\"单身\"这一身份，紧接着 the only life insurance he needs is enough to cover his\ndebts and burial expenses 直接给出该身份对应的需求，这句正面应答题干，就是答\n案句。\n【替换】cover his debts and burial expenses ↔ covers their debts and burial expenses\n几乎原句对应、仅人称改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nprovide for emergency needs 录音从未提到\"应急需求\"，单身者那句只说还债与丧葬，应\n急是凭常识想当然加上的。\nB) 偷换概念\npay for their medical expenses 把录音的\"丧葬费用\"偷换成\"医疗费用\"，二者都含\nexpenses 且都是开销，听到 expenses 就选会中招，但录音说的是 burial expenses（丧\n葬）而非医疗。\n借用原文：burial expenses\nD) 张冠李戴\nensures a healthy life for their later years 谈的是晚年健康保障，而录音对单身者只讲还\n债与丧葬；\"later years/退休\"是录音结尾给已成家者讲的内容，挪到单身者这题上属张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：the emphasis on family financial security will shift from protection to saving for the\nretirement years", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What should people do as their family increases in size?", "options": ["A) Purchase insurance for their children.", "B) Save sufficient money for a rainy day.", "C) Buy a home with a small down payment.", "D) Add more insurance on the breadwinner."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Add more insurance on the breadwinner.\n【定位】As the family increases in size, it is essential to add more insurance on the breadwinner to\nprotect the dependents.\n随着家庭规模变大，必须给家中的顶梁柱增加更多保险，以保障需要供养的家人。\n【信号】建议句 it is essential to 给出做法。录音里 As the family increases in size 复现了题干的\n情形，紧接 it is essential to add more insurance on the breadwinner 用\"必须做某\n事\"的建议口吻给出做法，这种 essential to 的强建议句就是答案信号。\n【替换】add more insurance on the breadwinner ↔ Add more insurance on the breadwinner\n原句基本照搬\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nPurchase insurance for their children 盗用了录音里 children 一词，但录音明说 most\npeople do not take out insurance on their young children，即一般不给小孩买保险，\n方向正相反。\n借用原文：most people do not take out insurance on their young children\nB) 答非所问\nSave sufficient money for a rainy day 讲的是存钱应急，录音在\"家庭变大\"这一情形下讲\n的是给顶梁柱加保，存钱（saving）是录音结尾讲孩子独立后才提的方向，搬到这题属答非\n所问。\n借用原文：the emphasis on family financial security will shift from protection to saving for the\nretirement years\nC) 原词陷阱\nBuy a home with a small down payment 几乎照搬录音 They have purchased a home\nwith a small down payment，但那只是描述这家人已发生的背景情况，并非\"家庭变大该\n做什么\"的建议，是典型听到原句就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：They have purchased a home with a small down payment", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "When should one change their life insurance?", "options": ["A) When their children grow up and leave home.", "B) When they have saved enough for retirement.", "C) When their family moves to a different place.", "D) When they have found better-paying jobs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） When their children grow up and leave home.\n【定位】As the children become financially independent of the family, the emphasis on family\nfinancial security will shift from protection to saving for the retirement years.\n当孩子在经济上独立、不再依赖家庭时，家庭财务保障的重心就会从\"提供保障\"转向\"为退休\n年月储蓄\"。\n【信号】转变信号 shift from…to 标出节点。录音用 As the children become financially\nindependent 给出时间条件，再用 will shift from protection to saving 表明保险重心在\n此刻发生转变，这个 shift from…to 的转变信号正是\"改变保险\"的时机所在，是答案句。\n【替换】As the children become financially independent of the family ↔\nWhen their children grow up and leave home\n孩子经济独立=长大离家的概括改写\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nsaved enough for retirement 盗用了录音里 retirement 一词，但录音说的是\"孩子独立\n时\"重心转向 saving for the retirement years，触发转变的是孩子独立，而非\"已存够退休\n金\"，把结果当成了时机。\n借用原文：saving for the retirement years\nC) 未提及\nfamily moves to a different place 说的是搬家，录音通篇讲的是家庭人口与孩子成长阶段\n的变化，从未提到搬到别处，属凭空添加。\nD) 未提及\nfound better-paying jobs 说的是找到更高薪的工作，录音讲的是孩子经济独立这一节点，\n并未把改变保险挂在\"换到高薪工作\"上，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about stereotypes?", "options": ["A) They do more harm than good.", "B) They have often been ignored.", "C) They do not help build friendship.", "D) They may not always be negative."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They may not always be negative.\n【定位】Also, stereotypes are often positive, particularly of groups that we ourselves belong to.\n而且，刻板印象往往是正面的，尤其是对我们自己所属的群体而言。\n【信号】极性反转的定调句。开篇先抛 \"Stereotype\" may sound like a bad word, but there's\nnothing bad about it.，再到 stereotypes are often positive——\"听着像坏词、其实并无\n不妥、往往是正面的\"这条极性反转线就是答案信号，说明它\"未必是负面的\"。\n【替换】stereotypes are often positive ↔ may not always be negative\n肯定正面 = 否定\"总是负面\"\nthere's nothing bad about it ↔ may not always be negative\n\"并无不妥\"印证\"未必负面\"\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey do more harm than good. 是\"弊大于利\"的负面定论，而录音明说 there's nothing\nbad about it，立场正好相反。\n借用原文：there's nothing bad about it\nB) 未提及\nThey have often been ignored. 说它\"常被忽视\"，但全篇讲的是其准确性、来源、影响，\n从未提到\"被忽视\"，属凭空添加。\nC) 未提及\nThey do not help build friendship. 看似贴合\"群体关系\"的话题，但录音谈的是\"我们自己\n所属群体\"的正面刻板印象，从没谈\"是否有助于建立友谊\"，是顺着话题硬接的无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What leads to the bias of stereotypes?", "options": ["A) Biased sources of information.", "B) Ignorance of cultural differences.", "C) Misinterpretation of Shakespeare.", "D) Tendency to jump to conclusions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Biased sources of information.\n【定位】But a lot of the information we get about human groups is through biased sources like how\nthey're represented in the media. And if these sources don't give you an accurate depiction,\nyour stereotype won't be accurate.\n但我们获得的很多关于人类群体的信息，是通过有偏的来源得来的，比如他们在媒体里被呈\n现的方式。如果这些来源给你的不是准确的描绘，你的刻板印象就不会准确。\n【信号】转折 But 后的归因句。前句说\"基于无偏样本时才可靠\"，紧接 But a lot of the\ninformation we get about human groups is through biased sources——转折 But 之\n后才是真正的成因落点，前半是铺垫。\n【替换】information we get about human groups is through biased sources ↔\nBiased sources of information\n同词根的语序重排\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIgnorance of cultural differences. 听上去与\"群体偏差\"相关，但录音把成因明确归到\"有偏\n来源\"，从未说\"无视文化差异\"导致偏差。\nC) 原词陷阱\nMisinterpretation of Shakespeare. 盗用了下一段 Shakespeare's depiction of Shylock\n的原词，但莎翁那段只是\"有偏来源\"的举例，且原文是\"被夏洛克塑造误导\"，不是\"误读莎士\n比亚\"，听到原词就选会落坑。\n借用原文：many Jews have been troubled by Shakespeare's depiction of Shylock\nD) 张冠李戴\nTendency to jump to conclusions. 与录音 we are good at drawing conclusions from\nthem 形似，但原文说人\"善于从刻板印象得出结论\"是中性能力陈述，并非\"急于下结论导致\n偏差\"，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：we are good at drawing conclusions from them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker say is a problem with stereotypes?", "options": ["A) They are hard to dismiss once attached to a certain group.", "B) They may have a negative impact on people they apply to.", "C) They persist even when circumstances have changed.", "D) They are often applied to minorities and ethnic people."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They may have a negative impact on people they apply to.\n【定位】A second problem is that stereotypes, regardless of whether or not they're accurate, can have a\nnegative effect on the people that they apply to.\n第二个问题是，刻板印象，不论它是否准确，都可能对被它套用的人产生负面影响。\n【信号】A second problem 明示的分论点句。A second problem is that stereotypes … can\nhave a negative effect on the people that they apply to——A second problem is\nthat 是作者亲口标出的\"问题\"，这种点题领起句就是答案信号。\n【替换】can have a negative effect on the people that they apply to ↔\nmay have a negative impact on people they apply to\neffect=impact 的近义替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey are hard to dismiss once attached to a certain group. 说它\"一旦贴上就难摆脱\"，\n全篇讲的是准确性、来源与负面影响，从未说\"难以去除\"，属凭空生造。\nC) 未提及\nThey persist even when circumstances have changed. 描述\"情况变了仍顽固\"，录音并\n无对应表述，是把刻板印象的常识印象当成原文内容。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey are often applied to minorities and ethnic people. 借后文 African-Americans 等\n少数群体的例子作联想，但录音说的是\"对被套用者(不限族群)的负面影响\"，把范围窄化\n成\"常用于少数族裔\"。\n借用原文：on the people that they apply to", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What did one recent study find about stereotypes?", "options": ["A) They impact people more or less in the same way.", "B) Some people are more sensitive to them than others.", "C) A positive stereotype may help one achieve better results.", "D) A negative stereotype sticks while a positive one does not"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A positive stereotype may help one achieve better results.\n【定位】When Asian-American women are given a test and they're asked to mark down their race,\nthey do better than they would otherwise do. They're reminded of a positive stereotype that\nboosts their morale.\n当亚裔美国女性参加测试、被要求标注种族时，她们会比平时考得更好。她们被唤起了一个\n正面的刻板印象，这提振了她们的士气。\n【信号】study found 后的发现句。One recent study found a sort of clever twist 领起后，紧跟\nthey do better than they would otherwise do 与 reminded of a positive stereotype\nthat boosts their morale——\"研究发现\"后面这组因果就是答案信号。\n【替换】reminded of a positive stereotype that boosts their morale ↔\nA positive stereotype may help one achieve better results\n提振士气→考得更好的概括\nthey do better than they would otherwise do ↔ achieve better results\n考得更好=取得更好结果\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey impact people more or less in the same way. 说影响\"对所有人大体相同\"，但研究\n恰恰发现正面与负面刻板印象作用相反、视提示内容而不同，与原文相反。\n借用原文：they do better than they would otherwise do\nB) 偷换概念\nSome people are more sensitive to them than others. 说\"有些人比别人更敏感\"，但研\n究讲的是同一批人受\"正面/负面\"不同提示而表现不同，不是\"人与人敏感度不同\"，偷换了比\n较对象。\nD) 与原文相反\nA negative stereotype sticks while a positive one does not. 盗用录音 stereotypes 与正\n负对照的语境，但研究明说正面刻板印象同样起作用(提振表现)，并非\"正面的不顶用\"，把\n结论说反了。\n借用原文：They're reminded of a positive stereotype that boosts their morale", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say you should do when you have an adverse reaction?", "options": ["A) Use some over-the-counter medicine instead.", "B) Quit taking the medicine immediately.", "C) Take some drug to relieve the side effect.", "D) Ask your pharmacist to explain why it occurs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Quit taking the medicine immediately.\n【定位】Whenever you have an adverse reaction, you should stop taking the drug right away.\n每当你出现不良反应时，应当立刻停止服用该药物。\n【信号】you should后的建议句。答案信号是建议句式 you should stop taking the drug right\naway——题干问“应该做什么”，录音里 you should 紧跟的那个动作就是答案落点；这正是\n预读时说的“抓 you should 后的动作”。\n【替换】stop taking the drug right away ↔ Quit taking the medicine immediately.\n同义替换换了词不换意\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 偷了录音里 over-the-counter 这个词（You should never use any over-the-counter\ndrug on a regular, continued basis）制造熟悉感，但那段讲的是“别长期乱用非处方药”，\n根本不是“出现不良反应时改用非处方药”，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：You should never use any over-the-counter drug on a regular, continued basis\nC) 答非所问\nC 借了录音里 relieve 一词（Ask your pharmacist whether he can suggest a drug that\nwill relieve the symptoms）显得耳熟，但录音说的是“先停药、再去问药剂师有没有能缓\n解症状的药”，是停药之后的后续步骤，不是题问的“出现不良反应当下该做什么”；先后顺序\n被偷换，且 C 没有提“停药”这个第一动作。\n借用原文：Ask your pharmacist whether he can suggest a drug that will relieve the symptoms\nD) 偷换概念\nD 抓住录音里的 pharmacist（Ask your pharmacist whether he can suggest a drug）\n造熟悉感，但录音让你问药剂师的是“能否推荐缓解症状的药”，而 D 篡改成“让药剂师解释\n为什么会发生（explain why it occurs）”，录音从未让你去问原因，属张冠李戴式改写。\n借用原文：Ask your pharmacist whether he can suggest a drug", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the speaker say about alcohol drinking?", "options": ["A) It may help patients fall asleep.", "B) It may lead to mental problems.", "C) It may cause serious harm to one's liver.", "D) It may increase the effect of certain drugs."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It may increase the effect of certain drugs.\n【定位】Alcohol may increase the effect of a drug—sleeping pills combine with alcohol to produce a\nsleepy feeling.\n酒精可能会增强药物的作用——安眠药与酒精同服会产生困倦感。\n【信号】锚词alcohol引出的后果句。答案信号是锚词 alcohol 直接领出的后果陈述 Alcohol may\nincrease the effect of a drug——题干以 alcohol 为锚，录音里这句开门见山讲它的作\n用，就是答案句；破折号后的安眠药只是例子。\n【替换】Alcohol may increase the effect of a drug ↔ It may increase the effect of certain drugs.\n近乎原句的等值改写\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nA 抓了录音破折号后 sleeping pills combine with alcohol to produce a sleepy feeling\n里的“犯困”造陷阱，但产生困意的主语是“安眠药+酒精一起”，且那只是举例说明酒精会增强\n药效；A 把例子里的现象（犯困）当成酒精本身的主作用，张冠李戴，并漏掉题问的核心\n“增强药效”。\n借用原文：sleeping pills combine with alcohol to produce a sleepy feeling\nB) 未提及\nB 说酒精“导致精神问题（mental problems）”，但全篇讲酒精只在第四段、只说它增强药\n效、产生困意，从未提到任何精神/心理问题，属凭印象添加的无中生有。\nC) 答非所问\nC 的“严重伤肝”看似呼应录音里的 kidney damage（excessive use of some painkilling\ndrugs may cause severe kidney damage），但那讲的是“过量止痛药伤肾”，与酒精无\n关，且器官是肾不是肝；C 把别段的药害硬安到酒精头上。\n借用原文：excessive use of some painkilling drugs may cause severe kidney damage", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker call on parents to do at the end of the talk?", "options": ["A) Tell their children to treat medicines with respect.", "B) Keep medicines out of the reach of their children.", "C) Make sure their children use quality medicines.", "D) Ask their children to use legitimate medicines."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Tell their children to treat medicines with respect.\n【定位】You can do your share to reduce the chances that your children will become part of the drug\nculture by treating all medicines with respect.\n你可以尽自己的一份力，通过郑重对待所有药物，来降低孩子卷入毒品文化的可能性。\n【信号】结尾you can的呼吁句。答案信号是结尾面向家长的呼吁句 You can do your share…by\ntreating all medicines with respect——题干问 at the end 呼吁家长做什么，录音末段\nyou can 引出的这句正是落点；by 后的 treating all medicines with respect 就是呼吁的\n具体做法。\n【替换】treating all medicines with respect ↔ Tell their children to treat medicines with respect.\n由家长自身做法收束到对孩子的告诫\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 的“把药放到孩子够不到处”是生活里最“正确”的防护做法，极易凭常识误选；但录音结尾\n呼吁的是“郑重对待药物、让孩子知道别乱用”，从未提到把药收起来/放高处，属常识替原文\n的无中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 借了录音的 medicines/children 等词显得耳熟，但把呼吁内容篡改成“确保孩子用优质药\n（quality medicines）”，录音通篇没提“药品质量好坏”，只讲“郑重/慎重对待药物”，所问\n之事被偷换。\n借用原文：Always let your children know that medicines and drugs should not be used carelessly\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 抓住录音第五段开头的 legitimate（adult abuse of legitimate medicines）造原词陷\n阱，但那是讲“成人滥用正规药与毒品文化有关”的背景，根本不是结尾呼吁家长做的事；D\n把背景词搬来拼成“让孩子用正规药”这一录音没说的呼吁。\n借用原文：adult abuse of legitimate medicines", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) permit\n动词\n【词性槽】will __ no fishing or mining：助动词 will + 空 + 宾语\n【依据】permit no fishing or mining = 不允许任何渔猎或采矿\n保护区的核心规则就是\"禁渔禁矿\"，permit no ... 的句式比 forbid 更地道、也最契合\nmarine sanctuary 的设立目的；且呼应下句 Palau also established the world's first\nshark sanctuary in 2009.。\n【竞争词】A) allocate 搭配错乱\nallocate \"分配\" 通常带 to 结构（allocate sth to sb），allocate no fishing 既不合搭配，也不\n能表达\"禁\"的含义。\nH) indulge 语义反向\nindulge 是\"纵容 / 沉溺\"，indulge no fishing 字面上像\"不纵容捕鱼\"，但实际惯用为 indulge\nin，与本句结构不匹配，且语义远不如 permit 干净。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) territory\n名词\n【词性槽】its maritime __：物主代词 + 形容词 + 空\n【依据】maritime territory = 海洋领土/海域\nmaritime territory 指一国的海洋领土；下句 The remaining 20 percent of the Palau\nseas 直接把 \"its maritime territory\" 等同于 \"Palau seas\"——\"海域\"正是 territory 在\nmaritime 限定下的含义。\n【竞争词】B) celebrities 类别完全不符\n把 80% 的\"海上名人\"留作保护区在逻辑上不成立。\nL) spectacle 可数性与语义偏离\nspectacle 多用于\"具体景象\"，与\"国家划出 80% 海洋面积\"的行政概念不在一个层面。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) exclusive\n形容词\n【词性槽】an __ economic zone：冠词 + 空 + 名词短语\n【依据】固定术语 exclusive economic zone（专属经济区）\n本句在讨论一个国家划给海洋保护的面积占比，所修饰对象正是国际法术语 exclusive\neconomic zone（专属经济区），这是把空锁死的固定搭配。\n【竞争词】C) commercial 搭配不成立\ncommercial economic zone 不是固定术语；下文 small-scale __(29)__ fishing businesses 才\n是 commercial 的归宿。\nE) essential 语义偏离\nessential 表示\"必不可少的\"，与 economic zone 搭配语义空泛，且与上下文\"专属经济区的占\n比\"这一具体行政概念不匹配。\nG) independent 概念错位\nindependent 多修饰国家、机构是否独立，independent economic zone 不是约定俗成的术\n语；它将留给第 32 空 an __(32)__ nation。\nK) solitary 感情色彩冲突\nsolitary 多用于人或事物\"孤零零\"的状态，与表示行政划界的 economic zone 完全不在一个语\n义场。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— independent 抢戏 exclusive。看到 an __(28)__\neconomic zone，若不熟 exclusive economic zone（专属经济区）这个国际法术语，主\n体考生很容易随手填 independent\"独立的经济区\"。防坑：把术语本身记下来，看到\neconomic zone 直接联想 exclusive。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) commercial\n形容词\n【词性槽】small-scale __ fishing businesses：形容词 + 空 + 名词\n【依据】与 with limited exports 呼应\n下文 with limited exports（出口受限）把这类企业定位为\"商业性渔业\"——commercial\nfishing businesses 是水产业里的固定术语，意指\"商业捕捞企业\"。\n【竞争词】K) solitary 感情色彩与对象不符\nsolitary fishing businesses\"孤单的渔业企业\"既不是术语，也无法解释 with limited exports\n这层商业含义。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) communities\n名词\n【词性槽】Island __ have been ...：形容词/名词 + 空 + 复数动词\n【依据】Island communities = 岛屿社区/岛民群体\nIsland communities 是地道说法，指\"靠海/居于岛上的人群\"；与下文 Creating this\nsanctuary is a bold move that the people of Palau recognise as __(31)__ to our\nsurvival. 中\"我们的生存\"指代关系吻合。\n【竞争词】B) celebrities 对象错位\nIsland celebrities\"岛上名人\"并非常见说法；且与\"受海洋威胁打击最重\"的群体不符——名人不\n会因海洋威胁就成为受害群体。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) essential\n形容词\n【词性槽】recognise ... as __ to our survival：as + 空 + to\n【依据】固定搭配 essential to survival\nessential to ...（对……至关重要）是高频搭配；与 our survival 组合后，语义\"对我们的生\n存至关重要\"与上文\"a bold move\"的果敢决策完全匹配。\n【竞争词】C) commercial 语义不通\ncommercial to our survival 既不是固定搭配，也无法解释\"商业的对生存\"这一逻辑。\nG) independent 搭配错误\nindependent of/from 才是惯用搭配；independent to our survival 不成立。\nK) solitary 语义错位\nsolitary 强调孤单、独自，与\"对生存至关重要\"的褒义判断不在同一频道。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) independent\n形容词\n【词性槽】has only been an __ nation：be + 冠词 + 空 + 名词\n【依据】an independent nation 是常见搭配\n全篇反复出现 nation 形容帕劳是一个新兴的太平洋岛国；an independent nation\"独立国\n家\"既符合\"独立才二十年\"的史实，also 与下句\"环保历史\"对接自然。\n【竞争词】C) commercial 概念不沾边\na commercial nation 不符合本句讨论\"建国年限\"的语境，把帕劳定性为\"商业国\"也与全文环保\n主旨相悖。\nK) solitary 感情色彩偏负\nsolitary 带\"孤零零\"意味，描述国家\"独立建国\"完全用不上，且与下文积极的环保叙事不协调。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) sponsor\n名词\n【词性槽】lead __ of the Palau National Marine Sanctuary Act：形容词 + 空 + of 短语\n【依据】lead sponsor of a bill/act = 法案的首席发起人\n在英美立法语境里，sponsor 指\"提案议员/法案发起人\"；lead sponsor of the ... Act 是固\n定政治术语，与\"Senator Hokkons Baules\"这个参议员身份完全匹配。\n【竞争词】B) celebrities 复数 + 概念错位\ncelebrities 是复数名词，前面 lead 形容词 + 单数冠位（同位语没有 a/an，常用单数）不匹\n配；\"名人\"也与立法发起人身份不沾。\nL) spectacle 对象错位\nspectacle 指\"奇观\"，不能作\"法案的领头人\"。\nO) territory 概念不对\nterritory 指\"领土、领地\"，与\"法案首席发起人\"的角色身份毫不相关。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) secure\n形容词\n【词性槽】build a __ future：动词 + 冠词 + 空 + 名词\n【依据】a secure future 是熟搭配且呼应主旨\na secure future for the Palauan people \"为帕劳人民构建一个有保障的未来\"是固定式表\n达；与上文 restoring the health of the ocean for future generations 的\"为后代\"主旨直\n接呼应。\n【竞争词】C) commercial 感情色彩与主旨冲突\ncommercial future 把\"未来\"商业化，与全篇\"保护、传统、可持续\"的基调正相反。\nK) solitary 情感方向相反\nsolitary 暗含\"孤独的\"，把帕劳人的未来描述成\"孤单的未来\"既不积极、也不符合 honoring\nconservation traditions 的语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) allocate", "B) celebrities", "C) commercial", "D) communities", "E) essential", "F) exclusive", "G) independent", "H) indulge", "I) permit", "J) secure", "K) solitary", "L) spectacle", "M) sponsor", "N) stocks", "O) territory"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) stocks\n名词\n【词性槽】give fish __ an opportunity：动词 + 名词 + 空 + 名词短语\n【依据】fish stocks = 鱼类种群，固定术语\nfish stocks 是水产/生态领域的固定术语，意为\"鱼类资源、鱼群存量\"；后接 an\nopportunity to replenish (补充)，正是 \"让鱼类种群有机会补充\"，与 \"bul\" 暂停捕捞的因\n果完全吻合。\n【竞争词】B) celebrities 话题完全不沾\ncelebrities 是\"名人\"，与鱼群、捕鱼语境毫无关系。\nD) communities 已留给第 30 空\ncommunities 用作\"群体\"时，要么指\"岛屿社区\"——更适配 Island __(30)__，本空与 fish 复合\n得到 fish communities 并非固定术语，远不及 fish stocks 地道。\nL) spectacle 语义偏离\nspectacle\"奇观、景象\"与\"让鱼类种群有机会补充\"语义不搭。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 D) —— communities 想冒充 stocks。give fish __(35)__ an\nopportunity to replenish(补充)，若不熟 fish stocks，主体考生可能想到 fish\ncommunities\"鱼群\"。防坑：替换法念一遍——\"给鱼类群体一个补充的机会\" 听上去虽\n通，但远不如\"鱼类种群得以补充\"的术语化表达地道；且 communities 已留给第 30 空。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Astronomer David Hogg doesn't think scooping is as serious a problem as generally thought.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】专名 David Hogg + scooping\n【定位】Hogg says that scooping is less of a problem than many think. \"The two cases I'm familiar\nwith didn't involve open data or code,\" he says.\nHogg 说，被抢先并不像很多人认为的那样严重。他说：我熟悉的那两起案例都与开放数据\n或代码无关。\n【改写】Astronomer David Hogg ↔ Hogg\n专名原样复现\ndoesn't think scooping is as serious a problem as generally thought ↔\nscooping is less of a problem than many think\n比较句式的同义改写\n【辨邻】 G G 段 Everybody has a scary story about someone getting scooped 是借 Hogg\n之口转述他人恐惧，与陈述句 doesn't think scooping is as serious a problem as\ngenerally thought 的弱化态度方向相反，不是源头段。\nEverybody has a scary story about someone getting scooped", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Some researchers are hesitant to make their data public for fear that others might publish something similar before them.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】怕别人抢先发表\n【定位】\"Everybody has a scary story about someone getting scooped,\" says New York University\nastronomer David Hogg. Those fears may be a factor in a lingering hesitation to share data\neven when publishing in journals that mandate it.\n纽约大学天文学家 David Hogg 说，每个人都有一个关于别人被抢先的可怕故事。这些恐惧\n可能是即使在强制要求分享数据的期刊上发表论文也仍然犹豫不决的一个原因。\n【改写】hesitant to make their data public ↔ a lingering hesitation to share data\n形容词转名词的同义替换\nfor fear that others might publish something similar before them ↔\nThose fears may be a factor in a lingering hesitation to share data\n用术语 scooped 概括短语\n【辨邻】 M M 段 scooping is less of a problem than many think 是在弱化 scoop 引发的恐\n惧，与陈述句 for fear that others might publish something similar before them\n的 担忧 方向相反，因此不是源头段。\nscooping is less of a problem than many think", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Some psychology journals have offered incentives to encourage authors to share their data.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】心理学期刊 + 激励\n【定位】A few psychology journals have created incentives to increase interest in reproducible science\n—for example, by affixing an 'open-data' badge to articles that clearly state where data\nare available.\n一些心理学期刊设立了激励机制以提升人们对可重复科学的兴趣——例如，给清楚说明数据\n出处的论文贴上一枚开放数据徽章。\n【改写】Some psychology journals ↔ A few psychology journals\n数量词同义\nhave offered incentives to encourage authors to share their data ↔\nhave created incentives to increase interest in reproducible science—for example, by affixing\nan 'open-data' badge to articles that clearly state where data are available\n动词同义+例证支撑\n【辨邻】 E E 段 Funders, too, are increasingly adopting an open-data policy 的主语是资助\n方，与题干 Some psychology journals 的主体不符。\nFunders, too, are increasingly adopting an open-data policy. Several strongly encourage, and some\nrequire, a data-management plan that makes data available.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "There is a growing demand in the science community that research data be open to the public.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】科学界对数据公开的呼声日增\n【定位】It is a movement building steady momentum: a call to make research data, software code and\nexperimental methods publicly available and transparent. A spirit of openness is gaining\nacceptance in the science community, and is the only way, say advocates, to address a 'crisis'\nin science whereby too few findings are successfully reproduced.\n这是一场势头持续增强的运动：呼吁公开并透明化研究数据、软件代码和实验方法。开放精\n神正在科学界获得认可，倡导者称这是应对科研可重复性危机的唯一途径。\n【改写】a growing demand ↔ a movement building steady momentum\n增长趋势的同义替换\nin the science community ↔ in the science community\n短语原样复现\nthat research data be open to the public ↔\na call to make research data, software code and experimental methods publicly available\n公开化诉求的同义改写\n【辨邻】 B B 段 scientists disagree about how much and when they should share data 的核\n心是分歧，与陈述句 a growing demand 的 增长共识 焦点不同，不是源头段。\nscientists disagree about how much and when they should share data, and they debate whether\nsharing it is more likely to accelerate science and make it more robust", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Sharing data offers early-career researchers the chance to build a certain level of reputation.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】early-career + reputation\n【定位】In the end, sharing data, software and materials with colleagues can help an early-career\nresearcher to gain recognition—a crucial component of success.\n归根结底，与同行分享数据、软件和材料能帮助早期生涯研究者获得认可——这是成功的一\n项关键组成部分。\n【改写】Sharing data ↔ sharing data, software and materials with colleagues\n宾语上位简化\noffers early-career researchers the chance to build a certain level of reputation ↔\ncan help an early-career researcher to gain recognition—a crucial component of success\n动词+名词的双重同义\n【辨邻】 N N 段 level the playing field by gaining better access to crucial data 谈的是 拿到\n数据 的机会，与陈述句 build a certain level of reputation 不同方向。\nOpen science also offers junior researchers the chance to level the playing field by gaining better\naccess to crucial data.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Data sharing enables scientists to publish each step of their research work, thus leading to more citations.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】每一步发布 + more citations\n【定位】For example, when information is uploaded to a repository, a digital object identifier (DOI) is\nassigned. Scientists can use a DOI to publish each step of the research life cycle, not just the\nfinal paper. In so doing, they can potentially get three citations—one each for the data and\nsoftware, in addition to the paper itself.\n例如，当信息上传到存储库时，会被分配一个数字对象标识符（DOI）。科学家可以用 DOI\n为研究生命周期的每一步发表成果，而不仅是最终论文。这样他们最多可以获得三次引用\n——数据和软件各一次，加上论文本身一次。\n【改写】publish each step of their research work ↔ publish each step of the research life cycle\n短语原样复现\nthus leading to more citations ↔\nthey can potentially get three citations—one each for the data and software, in addition to the\npaper itself\n数字具体化为 more", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Scientists hold different opinions about the extent and timing of data sharing.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对分享 extent 与 timing 意见不一\n【定位】On the other hand, scientists disagree about how much and when they should share data, and\nthey debate whether sharing it is more likely to accelerate science and make it more robust, or\nto introduce vulnerabilities and problems.\n另一方面，科学家们对应该分享多少以及何时分享意见不一，并就分享究竟更可能加速科学\n并使其更稳健、还是引入漏洞与问题展开争论。\n【改写】Scientists hold different opinions ↔ scientists disagree\n态度同义\nabout the extent and timing of data sharing ↔\nabout how much and when they should share data\n疑问词名词化\n【辨邻】 K K 段 two camps in a field—those who care about open science and those who\ndon't 是合作意愿层面的分歧，与陈述句关心的 extent and timing 不在一个维度上。\ntwo camps in a field—those who care about open science and those who don't", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Potential problems related to data sharing should be made known to and discussed by all participants at the beginning of a joint research project.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】项目开头就讨论问题\n【定位】Concerns about open science should be discussed at the outset of a study. \"Whenever you\nstart a project with someone, you have to establish a clear understanding of expectations for\nwho owns the data, at what point they go public and who can do what with them,\" he says.\n对开放科学的担忧应在研究一开始就讨论。他说：每当你和别人启动一个项目时，都必须就\n数据归属、何时公开、谁可以做什么形成清晰的共识。\n【改写】Potential problems related to data sharing ↔ Concerns about open science\n上位概括同义\nmade known to and discussed by all participants ↔\nWhenever you start a project with someone, you have to establish a clear understanding of\nexpectations for who owns the data, at what point they go public and who can do what with\nthem\n讨论参与方的同义重构\nat the beginning of a joint research project ↔ at the outset of a study\n时间状语同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Sharing data and handling data-related issues can be time-consuming.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】分享与处理数据耗时\n【定位】Researchers also point to the time sink that is involved in preparing data for others to view.\nOnce the data and associated materials appear in a repository, answering questions and\nhandling complaints can take many hours.\n研究者们还指出，为他人查看而准备数据是一种时间消耗。一旦数据与相关材料出现在存储\n库中，回答问题和处理投诉可能要花很多个小时。\n【改写】Sharing data ↔ preparing data for others to view\n动作过程同义\nhandling data-related issues ↔ answering questions and handling complaints\n处理事项的同义具体化\ncan be time-consuming ↔ the time sink can take many hours\n耗时的同义表达\n【辨邻】 J J 段 The time investment can present other problems 引出的是 同行偏见，与陈述\n句 can be time-consuming 的焦点 时间本身 不同，因此不是源头段。\nThe time investment can present other problems", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Junior researchers may have no say when it comes to sharing data.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Junior researchers 无话语权\n【定位】Graduate students and postdocs, who often are working on their lab head's grant, may have no\nchoice if their supervisor or another senior colleague opposes sharing.\n研究生和博士后通常在他们实验室负责人的项目经费下工作，如果其导师或另一位高级同事\n反对分享，他们可能没有选择。\n【改写】Junior researchers ↔ Graduate students and postdocs\n上位词对下位群体\nmay have no say when it comes to sharing data ↔\nmay have no choice if their supervisor or another senior colleague opposes sharing\n话语权与选择权的同义\n【辨邻】 C C 段 They must determine the risk of jeopardizing a job offer or a collaboration\nproposal 主语是青年学者自己在权衡，与陈述句 no say 的 被动 不同方向。\nThey must determine the risk of jeopardizing a job offer or a collaboration proposal from those who\nare wary of—or unfamiliar with—open science.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What question does the example in the movie raise?", "options": ["A) Whether robots can reach better decisions.", "B) Whether robots follow Asimov's zeroth law.", "C) How robots may make bad judgments.", "D) How robots should be programmed."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What question does the example in the movie raise? 直接询问例子引出的具体问题，\n答案应是原文里那句问句的转述，属于细节定位题。\n【拆句】What question does the example in the movie raise? 主语 the example、谓语 raise、\n宾语 What question 提前作问；structure 平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The robot's decision and its calculated approach raise an important question: would humans\nmake the same choice? And which choice would we want our robotic counterparts to make?\n机器人的这一决策及其基于计算的处理方式提出了一个重要问题：人类是否会做出同样的选\n择？以及我们希望我们的机器人同伴做出怎样的选择？\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nWhether robots can reach better decisions. 把原文的\"人类会不会做同样选择\"偷换\n成\"机器人能否做更好的决定\"，引入了原文未提的\"更好\"这一比较。\nB) 张冠李戴\nWhether robots follow Asimov's zeroth law. 把第三段才出现的 zeroth law 硬塞到第\n一段问句里，定位错段。\nC) 无中生有\nHow robots may make bad judgments. 暗含\"机器人做了坏判断\"的负面定性，但原文\n只是中性描述\"按概率救人\"，并未称之为 bad judgments。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwhich choice would we want our robotic counterparts to make? ↔\nHow robots should be programmed.\n预判方向是\"机器人应如何被设定决策\"，How robots should be programmed 正是这\n一方向的最精炼上位概括，与定位句意图完全吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author think of Asimov's three laws of robotics?", "options": ["A) They are apparently divorced from reality.", "B) They did not follow the coding system of robotics.", "C) They laid a solid foundation for robotics.", "D) They did not take moral issues into consideration."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of Asimov's three laws of robotics? 问作者对三定律的具体\n看法，原文须给出明文评价，本质仍是细节定位+理解。\n【拆句】What does the author think of Asimov's three laws of robotics? 主谓宾 the author /\nthink of / X，疑问代词 What 前置；句式平实。\n【定位】Isaac Asimov evaded the whole notion of morality in devising his three laws of robotics,\nwhich hold that 1. Robots cannot harm humans or allow humans to come to harm; 2. Robots\nmust obey humans, except where the order would conflict with law 1; and 3. Robots must act\nin self-preservation, unless doing so conflicts with laws 1 or 2.\n艾萨克·阿西莫夫在设计他的机器人三大法则时回避了\"道德\"这一整个概念。这三条法则是：", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author say about Asimov's robots?", "options": ["A) They know what is good or bad for human beings.", "B) They are programmed not to hurt human beings.", "C) They perform duties in their owners' best interest.", "D) They stop working when a moral issue is involved."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about Asimov's robots? 问的是文章如何描述这群机器人，答\n案能在文本中找到对应原句。\n【拆句】What does the author say about Asimov's robots? 直问句，疑问代词 What 作 say 的\n宾语，about 引出对象；结构简单。\n【定位】These laws are programmed into Asimov's robots—they don't have to think, judge, or value.\nThey don't have to like humans or believe that hurting them is wrong or bad. They simply\ndon't do it.\n这些法则被编程进阿西莫夫笔下的机器人体内——它们不必思考、判断或评价。它们不必喜\n欢人类，也不必认同伤害人类是错或恶。它们只是\"不做\"而已。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey know what is good or bad for human beings. 与原文 They don't have to…\nbelieve that hurting them is wrong or bad 直接矛盾——原文说\"不必相信伤害人类\n是错或恶\"，即不需要知道善恶。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThese laws are programmed into Asimov's robots ↔\nThey are programmed not to hurt human beings.\nRobots cannot harm humans or allow humans to come to harm ↔ not to hurt human beings\n正解抓住\"被编程进去的就是不伤人\"这一最核心机制，是定位句和法则1合并后的同义\n改写。\nC) 无中生有\nThey perform duties in their owners' best interest. 引入\"主人最佳利益\"这一文中根\n本没有的角色，原文谈的是 humans/humanity 而非 owners。\nD) 过度推断\nThey stop working when a moral issue is involved. 描述了\"碰到道德问题就停工\"的\n情景，原文只说机器人不需思考道德，没有任何\"停工\"的描述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author want to say by mentioning the word \"harm\" in Asimov's laws?", "options": ["A) Abstract concepts are hard to program.", "B) It is hard for robots to make decisions.", "C) Robots may do harm in certain situations.", "D) Asimov's laws use too many vague terms."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the author want to say by mentioning the word \"harm\" in Asimov's laws?\n明确问\"提到 harm 这个词意在说明什么\"，是典型例证目的题。\n【拆句】What does the author want to say by mentioning the word \"harm\" in Asimov's laws?\n主干 the author want to say what；by mentioning… 是方式状语；in Asimov's laws 修\n饰 harm，定位范围更精确。\n【定位】A word such as \"harm\" is vague (what about emotional harm? Is replacing a human employee\nharm?), and abstract concepts present coding problems.\n像\"伤害\"这样的词含义模糊（情感上的伤害算不算？取代一名人类员工算不算伤害？），而\n抽象概念在编程上会带来问题。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nabstract concepts present coding problems ↔ Abstract concepts are hard to program.\n预判方向是\"抽象概念难编程\"，A 几乎是 abstract concepts present coding\nproblems 的逐词改写，是例证目的题的标准答案形态。\nB) 答非所问\nIt is hard for robots to make decisions. 谈的是机器人决策困难，是后续 P5 实验的结\n论，而非 harm 这个例子的目的。\nC) 偷换概念\nRobots may do harm in certain situations. 谈机器人\"在某些情境下会造成伤害\"，混\n淆了 harm 例子的服务对象——这里 harm 是被讨论的词，不是机器人行为本身。\nD) 以偏概全\nAsimov's laws use too many vague terms. 把\"举一个模糊词 harm\"夸大成\"使用太多\n模糊术语\"，原文只举一例并未做\"too many\"的程度判定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What has the roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory found in his experiment?", "options": ["A) Robots can be made as intelligent as human beings some day.", "B) Robots can have moral issues encoded into their programs.", "C) Robots can have trouble making decisions in complex scenarios.", "D) Robots can be programmed to perceive potential perils."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What has the roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory found in his experiment?\n问的是实验具体发现，答案可直接在原文找到事实陈述。\n【拆句】What has the roboticist at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory found in his experiment?\n现在完成时 has…found，强调发现这一动作的结果；at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory\n作 the roboticist 的后置定语，缩小指代范围。\n【定位】But when two H-bots became imperiled, the robot choked 42 percent of the time, unable to\ndecide which to save and letting them both \"die.\"\n但当两个 H-bot 同时陷入险境时，该机器人有 42% 的概率\"卡壳\"——它无法决定先救哪一\n个，结果让两个都\"死\"了。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nRobots can be made as intelligent as human beings some day. 提到\"将来\"和\"和人\n类一样聪明\"，原文实验只给当下结果，未做任何未来预测。\nB) 与原文相反\nRobots can have moral issues encoded into their programs. 说\"可以把道德议题编\n码进程序\"，与原文 The experiment highlights the importance of morality——之所\n以强调道德重要性恰是因为现在没编码进去——的逻辑相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwhen two H-bots became imperiled, the robot choked 42 percent of the time, unable to decide\nwhich to save\n↔ Robots can have trouble making decisions in complex scenarios.\n选项把\"两个 H-bot 同时遇险卡壳\"的具体结果，抽象为\"复杂场景下做决策困难\"，是细\n节题最规范的同义改写。\nD) 偷换概念\nRobots can be programmed to perceive potential perils. 把\"被编程救人于险\"偷换\n为\"被编程感知风险\"，但实验真正的发现是\"在两难情境会卡壳\"，而非\"能否感知风险\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the example of iPhone 6s serve to show?", "options": ["A) The huge capacity of the smartphones people now use.", "B) The widespread use of smartphones all over the world.", "C) The huge impact of new technology on people's everyday life.", "D) The rapid technological progress in a very short period of time."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the example of iPhone 6s serve to show? 明确问\"这个例子用来说明什么\"，\n是典型的例证目的题。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What does the example serve to show，of iPhone 6s 是 example 的后置\n定语，serve to do 意为\"用来做某事\"。句子平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Our world now moves so fast that we seldom stop to see just how far we have come in just a\nfew years. The latest iPhone 6s, for example, has a dual-core processor and fits nicely into\nyour pocket. By comparison, you would expect to find a technological specification like this\non your standard laptop in an office anywhere in the world.\n我们的世界如今变化太快，我们几乎不停下来看一看：仅仅几年间我们已经走出了多远。比\n如最新的 iPhone 6s 配有双核处理器，又能轻松装进口袋；相比之下，你本以为这种技术规\n格要在世界各地办公室里的标准笔记本电脑上才能见到。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nThe huge capacity of the smartphones people now use. 只复述了例子里 dual-core\nprocessor 这种\"容量大\"的细节，没有上升到作者真正要论证的\"技术进步快\"。\nB) 无中生有\nThe widespread use of smartphones all over the world. 谈的是\"普及程度\"，可原文\niPhone 6s 例子只谈\"技术规格\"，全段也没提\"在全世界被广泛使用\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe huge impact of new technology on people's everyday life. 把话题偷换成\"新技\n术对日常生活的巨大影响\"，可原文 iPhone 6s 例子只谈\"几年前到现在的技术规格变\n化\"，没有展开\"对日常生活造成何种影响\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nOur world now moves so fast that we seldom stop to see just how far we have come in just a few\nyears.\n↔ The rapid technological progress in a very short period of time.\n预判\"短时间内技术进步惊人\"与 The rapid technological progress in a very short\nperiod of time. 完全对位，rapid = so fast、in a very short period of time = in just\na few years。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What can we expect to see by the year 2020?", "options": ["A) Apps for the Internet of Things.", "B) The popularization of smart homes.", "C) The emergence of Millennials.", "D) Total globalization of the world."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What can we expect to see by the year 2020? 是问\"2020 年会看到什么\"，答案就在原\n文那句话里，不需推断。\n【拆句】What 作 see 的宾语，we 是主语，can expect to see 是谓语，by the year 2020 是时间\n状语；句式简单。\n【定位】All researchers agree that close to 25 billion devices, things and sensors will be connected by\n2020 which incidentally is also the moment that Millennials(千禧一代) are expected to make\nup 75 percent of our overall workforce, and the fully connected home will become a reality\nfor large numbers of people worldwide.\n所有研究者一致认为，到 2020 年将有近 250 亿台设备、物品和传感器接入互联网；2020\n年也恰是千禧一代预计将占整体劳动力 75% 的时刻；而\"全互联家庭\"也将在全球范围内为\n大量人群所拥有。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nApps for the Internet of Things. 来自第 2 段 new applications for the Internet of\nThings are moving ahead fast，是现在进行时\"正在快速推进\"，并非 2020 才能看\n到；时间不匹配。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe fully connected home will become a reality for large numbers of people worldwide ↔\nThe popularization of smart homes.\nThe popularization of smart homes. 用 popularization 概括 become a reality for\nlarge numbers of people，用 smart homes 概括 fully connected home，完全\n等值。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe emergence of Millennials. 用 emergence（出现/兴起）形容千禧一代，可原文\nMillennials(千禧一代) are expected to make up 75 percent of our overall\nworkforce 说的是\"届时占劳动力 75%\"，不是\"届时才出现\"——千禧一代早已存在。\nD) 过度推断\nTotal globalization of the world. 中的 total globalization\"完全全球化\"是原文从未出\n现的概念；定位句虽含 worldwide 一词，但是修饰\"全互联家庭普及范围\"，不等于\"全\n世界已彻底全球化\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What will business owners do when they become aware of the benefits of the Internet of Things?", "options": ["A) Employ fewer workers in their operations.", "B) Gain automatic control of their businesses.", "C) Invest in more smart buildings and cities.", "D) Embrace whatever new technology there is."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What will business owners do when they become aware of the benefits of the\nInternet of Things? 问的是企业主一旦意识到好处会采取什么行动，答案是原文里的客观\n陈述。\n【拆句】主干 What will business owners do，when 引导时间状语从句，从句里 they 回指\nbusiness owners，become aware of \"意识到\"。注意 when 在这里是\"一旦/当……时\"，不\n是疑问词。\n【定位】However, this is just the tip of the proverbial iceberg as smart buildings and even cities\nincreasingly become the norm as leaders and business owners begin to wake up to the\nmassive savings that technology can deliver through connected sensors and new forms of\nautomation coupled with intelligent energy and facilities management.\n然而，这只是冰山一角——随着领导者和企业主开始意识到技术可通过互联传感器、新型自\n动化以及智能能源与设施管理带来的巨大节约，智能楼宇乃至智能城市正越来越成为常态。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nEmploy fewer workers in their operations. 把 massive savings\"巨大节约\"理解成\"裁\n员省钱\"，可原文只说技术通过 sensors、automation、智能能源/设施管理带来节约，\n从未提及\"减少员工\"。\nB) 偷换概念\nGain automatic control of their businesses. 把 new forms of automation 这一\"技\n术手段\"曲解成\"业主获得对企业的自动控制\"。原文 automation 是技术带来节约的方式\n之一，业主并不会因此\"获得对生意的自动控制\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nsmart buildings and even cities increasingly become the norm ↔\nInvest in more smart buildings and cities.\nas leaders and business owners begin to wake up to the massive savings that technology can\ndeliver\n↔ Invest in more smart buildings and cities.\n预判\"推动智能楼宇/城市变成常态\"与 Invest in more smart buildings and cities. 完全\n对位：invest in more 正是让 smart buildings and even cities ... become the norm\n这一普及结果发生的业主侧动作。\nD) 以偏概全\nEmbrace whatever new technology there is. 把对象从 smart buildings and even\ncities 这个具体范畴扩大成 whatever new technology there is\"凡是新技术全都拥\n抱\"，明显超出原文。原文只说业主意识到\"这门技术\"带来节约后让\"智能楼宇/城市\"成为\n常态，没有承诺\"对所有新技术来者不拒\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is the most exciting challenge when we possess more and more data?", "options": ["A) How to turn it to profitable use.", "B) How to do real time data analysis.", "C) How to link the actionable systems.", "D) How to devise new ways to store it."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the most exciting challenge when we possess more and more data? 问\"最激\n动人心的挑战是什么\"，答案就在原文那句\"最大的挑战是……\"里。\n【拆句】主干 What is the most exciting challenge，when 引导时间状语从句\"当我们拥有越来越\n多数据时\"。more and more 表数量递增。\n【定位】The biggest and most exciting challenge of this technology is how to creatively leverage this\never-growing amount of data to deliver cost savings, improvements and tangible benefits to\nboth businesses and citizens of these smart cities.\n这项技术最大、也最激动人心的挑战，是如何创造性地利用这些不断增长的数据，为智能城\n市中的企业与市民带来成本节约、改善与切实可感的收益。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhow to creatively leverage this ever-growing amount of data to deliver cost savings,\nimprovements and tangible benefits\n↔ How to turn it to profitable use.\n预判\"把海量数据创造性地变成实际效益\"与 How to turn it to profitable use. 完全对\n位，turn it to profitable use = leverage data to deliver benefits。\nB) 张冠李戴\nHow to do real time data analysis. 来自 run real time data analytics to extract\nactionable and ongoing knowledge，是上一段铺垫的能力，而第 7 段明确把\"最大挑\n战\"定义为 how to creatively leverage ... data，不是\"如何做实时分析\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nHow to link the actionable systems. 用 link the actionable systems\"连接可行动的\n系统\"，原文 actionable and ongoing knowledge 里 actionable 修饰 knowledge\n（可付诸行动的知识），并非什么 actionable systems；同时\"如何连接系统\"也不是原\n文设定的挑战。\nD) 答非所问\nHow to devise new ways to store it. 中的 store data\"存储数据\"来自 the housing of\nthis enormous and rapidly growing amount of data，但原文紧接着用 not only ...\nbut also ... 把重点放在\"运行实时数据分析\"上，第 7 段更把\"最大挑战\"定义为\"创造性利\n用数据\"，存储显然不是答案。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author think about working from anywhere and at anytime?", "options": ["A) It is feasible with a connection to the internet.", "B) It will thrive in smart buildings, cities and homes.", "C) It is still a distant Utopian dream for ordinary workers.", "D) It will deliver tangible benefits to both boss and worker."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干虽含 think，但锁死了具体话题 working from anywhere and at anytime，作者的看\n法就在那一两句话里，仍走定位路径而非全文串线。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author think about X，about 后跟动名词短语 working from\nanywhere and at anytime；anywhere 和 anytime 由 and 并列。\n【定位】Let's face it, it wasn't too long ago that the idea of working from anywhere and at anytime\nwas some form of a distant Utopian(乌托邦式的) dream, and yet now we can perform almost\nany office-based task from any location in the world as long as we have access to the internet.\n我们得承认，不久以前\"任何时间任何地点办公\"还像是遥远的乌托邦梦；可如今只要我们能\n上网，就几乎能在世界上任何地方完成几乎任何办公任务。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nand yet now we can perform almost any office-based task from any location in the world as long\nas we have access to the internet\n↔ It is feasible with a connection to the internet.\n预判\"只要上网就能实现\"与 It is feasible with a connection to the internet. 完全对\n位：feasible 概括\"can perform\"\"已经能做到\"，with a connection to the internet 等\n值于 as long as we have access to the internet。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt will thrive in smart buildings, cities and homes. 把 smart buildings, cities and\nhomes 这一全文话题硬粘到\"任意时间地点办公\"上。原文 P8 明确给出的条件是 access\nto the internet，并没有说\"在智能楼宇/城市/家里才会兴盛\"。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt is still a distant Utopian dream for ordinary workers. 把作者明确否定的旧状态当\n成现状。原文以 and yet now 转折，已说明 distant Utopian dream 是 \"过去的看\n法\"，作者现在的态度恰恰相反；这是典型\"正话反说\"。\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt will deliver tangible benefits to both boss and worker. 中的 tangible benefits 来\n自第 7 段 cost savings, improvements and tangible benefits to both businesses\nand citizens，谈的是\"用数据带来收益\"的对象，被搬到\"任意时间地点办公\"上做承诺；\n同时 boss/worker 这种二分对象也是原文没有的。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
